/* =====================================================
   FUGA MYTHICA — ETYM: root-origin stories
   key -> [zhStory, enStory]
   讲词根从哪里来、怎么演变出今天的意思;不罗列衍生词(表格负责)。
   ===================================================== */
const ETYM = {
  /* ---- C1 L1 ---- */
  pater:["六千年前的印欧牧人喊父亲为 pa——婴儿最早发出的音之一。加上亲属词尾 -ter,就成了横跨欧亚的\"父亲\":梵语 pitar、希腊 pater、拉丁 pater 是同一声呼唤。","Six thousand years ago Indo-European herders called father pa — one of a baby's first sounds. With kinship ending -ter it became the word that crossed Eurasia: Sanskrit pitar, Greek pater, Latin pater — one same call."],
  mater:["和 pater 一样古老:ma 是婴儿吮吸时最容易发的音,加 -ter 成\"母亲\"。几乎地球上每种语言的\"妈妈\"都从 ma 开始——这不是巧合,是婴儿的嘴决定的。","As old as pater: ma is the easiest sound a nursing baby makes; with -ter it became mother. Nearly every language's mama begins with ma — decided not by history but by babies' lips."],
  filius:["来自古老词根 dhe(i)-(吮吸、哺乳):filius 本义是\"吃奶的孩子\"。对罗马人来说,儿子首先是母亲怀里喂大的那个小人儿。","From ancient root dhe(i)- (to suckle): a filius is literally \"the nursling.\" To Romans a son was first of all the little one fed at his mother's breast."],
  filia:["filius 的阴性——把 -us 换成 -a 就从儿子变成女儿。拉丁语用一个元音区分性别,这个 -a 后来传遍欧洲女性名字:Maria、Julia、Aemilia。","Feminine of filius — swap -us for -a and son becomes daughter. That little -a spread through Europe's female names: Maria, Julia, Aemilia."],
  rex:["词根 reg- 本义\"划直线、引导\":国王就是\"给部落划定方向的人\"。古罗马最恨这个词——赶走末代国王后,谁敢称 rex 谁就得死。","Root reg- meant \"to draw a straight line, to guide\": a king is he who sets the tribe's direction. Rome came to hate the word — after the last king fell, claiming it meant death."],
  regina:["rex 的阴性形式。罗马共和国没有女王,这个词主要留给神界:天后朱诺被称为 Regina——诸神的王后。","Feminine of rex. Republican Rome had no queens, so the word belonged to heaven: Juno was Regina, queen of the gods."],
  puer:["本义\"小的、幼的\",与 parvus(小)远亲。罗马学校里老师一开口就是 pueri!(孩子们!)——两千年前的课堂喊声。","Originally \"small, young,\" distant kin of parvus. Roman teachers opened class with pueri! (\"children!\") — a classroom call two thousand years old."],
  puella:["puer 加上昵称词尾 -ella:\"小小孩\"→女孩。罗马诗人卡图卢斯用 puella 称呼他的爱人——既是\"女孩\",也是\"亲爱的\"。","puer plus pet-ending -ella: \"little child\" → girl. The poet Catullus called his beloved puella — both \"girl\" and \"darling.\""],
  femina:["来自词根 dhe(i)-(哺乳),本义\"哺育者\"——和 filius(吃奶者)是一对:一个喂,一个吃。语言把母亲和孩子拴在同一个词根上。","From root dhe(i)- (to suckle): \"she who nurses\" — paired with filius, \"he who is nursed.\" One root ties mother and child together."],
  vir:["印欧词根 wiro-(男人/战士),和英语 werewolf 的 were- 同源——werewolf 就是\"人狼\"。罗马人由它引出 virtus:男子汉该有的一切品质。","Indo-European wiro- (man, warrior) — the were- of werewolf is the same word: \"man-wolf.\" From it Rome built virtus: everything a man ought to be."],
  frater:["印欧亲属称谓 bhrater 的拉丁形——英语 brother、梵语 bhratar、拉丁 frater 是同一个词走了三条路。后来修道院的\"修士\"也叫 frater:上帝之家的兄弟。","Latin shape of Indo-European bhrater — brother, Sanskrit bhratar and frater are one word down three roads. Later monks were called frater too: brothers in God's house."],
  soror:["印欧 swesor(姐妹)的拉丁后代,英语 sister 是它的日耳曼堂亲。s 开头、r 收尾的骨架四千年没变。","Latin child of Indo-European swesor; English sister is its Germanic cousin. The s...r skeleton hasn't changed in four thousand years."],
  amicus:["动词 amare(爱)加\"关系人\"词尾 -icus:朋友=你所爱的人。罗马人交友即谈爱,西塞罗写《论友谊》通篇都在讲 amor 与 amicus 的血缘。","Verb amare (to love) plus relation-ending -icus: a friend is one you love. Cicero's essay On Friendship is all about the kinship of amor and amicus."],
  populus:["本是军事词:\"集结起来的武装公民\"。罗马国名缩写 SPQR 里的 P 就是它——Senatus Populusque Romanus,元老院与罗马人民。","Originally military: \"the armed citizenry assembled.\" The P in Rome's own name SPQR is this word — Senatus Populusque Romanus, the Senate and People of Rome."],
  deus:["印欧词根 dyeu-(明亮的天空):最早的神就是白昼的天光。宙斯 Zeus、朱庇特 Iu-piter(天父)、deus,全是同一片亮天。","Indo-European dyeu- (the bright sky): the first god WAS daylight itself. Zeus, Iu-piter (sky-father) and deus are all the same shining sky."],
  dea:["deus 的阴性。罗马神庙铭文给女神冠以 DEA 开头——DEA ROMA(罗马女神)、DEA FORTUNA(命运女神)。","Feminine of deus. Temple inscriptions open with DEA — DEA ROMA, DEA FORTUNA."],
  heros:["希腊语借词:荷马史诗里 heros 指\"半神的勇士\"——比人强,比神弱,注定要用凡人之躯做不朽之事。","A Greek loan: in Homer a heros is the half-divine warrior — more than man, less than god, doomed to do deathless deeds in mortal flesh."],
  nomen:["印欧 nomen(名字)几乎原样活了五千年:梵语 naman、希腊 onoma、拉丁 nomen。罗马人说 nomen est omen——名字即预兆。","Indo-European nomen has lived almost unchanged for five millennia: Sanskrit naman, Greek onoma, Latin nomen. Romans said nomen est omen — the name is a sign."],
  familia:["原义并不温馨:famulus 是\"仆人\",familia 指一家之主名下的全部人口——妻儿、仆从、奴隶。先是财产清单,后来才变成\"家\"。","Not cozy at first: famulus meant servant, and familia was everyone under the master's roof — wife, children, servants, slaves. A property list before it became \"family.\""],
  domus:["印欧 dom-(建造)→\"建起来的东西\"=房子。主人是 dominus,统治是 dominari——罗马人的权力观从自家屋顶开始。","Indo-European dom- (to build) → \"the built thing\" = house. Master is dominus, to rule is dominari — Roman power began under one's own roof."],
  /* ---- C1 L2 ---- */
  navis:["印欧 nau-(船):梵语 nau、希腊 naus、拉丁 navis——人类最古老的交通词之一,比轮子的词根还整齐。","Indo-European nau- (boat): Sanskrit nau, Greek naus, Latin navis — one of humanity's oldest transport words, tidier even than the wheel's."],
  aqua:["古意大利本土词,本义就是\"活水\"——能喝、能灌溉的水;海水另有其名(mare)。罗马引水渠让 aqua 成了文明的度量。","A native Italic word for living water — water you can drink and channel; the sea had its own name (mare). Rome's aqueducts made aqua the measure of civilization."],
  mare:["印欧 mori-(水面):英语 mere(小湖)、德语 Meer 都是亲戚。罗马人把地中海骄傲地叫作 Mare Nostrum——\"我们的海\"。","Indo-European mori- (standing water): English mere and German Meer are kin. Romans called the Mediterranean Mare Nostrum — \"Our Sea.\""],
  ventus:["印欧 we-(吹)的分词形:\"正在吹的东西\"。英语 wind、德语 Wind 同出一口气。","Participle of Indo-European we- (to blow): \"that which is blowing.\" English wind is the same breath."],
  caelum:["词源成谜,或与 caelare(雕刻)有关——罗马人想象天空是一只倒扣的、刻满星辰的碗。","Origin uncertain — perhaps from caelare (to engrave): Romans imagined the sky as an upturned bowl carved with stars."],
  stella:["印欧 ster-(星)加小词尾:\"小星星\"。希腊 aster、英语 star、拉丁 stella——全人类仰望的是同一个词根。","Indo-European ster- (star) with a diminutive: \"little star.\" Greek aster, English star, Latin stella — all mankind looks up at one root."],
  sol:["印欧 sawel-(太阳)的拉丁形;英语 sun 是同根的日耳曼支。罗马晚期把 12 月 25 日定为\"不可战胜的太阳\"(Sol Invictus)的生日。","Latin shape of Indo-European sawel-; English sun is the Germanic branch. Late Rome kept December 25 as the birthday of Sol Invictus, the Unconquered Sun."],
  luna:["由 louksna(发光的)缩来——月亮是\"夜里发光的那个\"。和 lux(光)同根:月光本来就是光的名字。","Contracted from louksna, \"the shining one\" — the moon is what glows by night. Same root as lux: moonlight was always a name of light."],
  nauta:["希腊 nautes(船员)借入拉丁语。希腊人是地中海最早的远航者,罗马连\"水手\"这个词都是向他们借的。","Greek nautes (crewman) borrowed into Latin. The Greeks sailed first, so Rome borrowed even its word for sailor from them."],
  insula:["词源不明,一说 in salo(在海里)——\"泡在海里的土地\"。罗马城里的多层公寓楼也叫 insula:陆地上的孤岛。","Origin unclear — one guess: in salo, \"in the sea\": land soaking in salt water. Rome's apartment blocks were also insulae: islands on land."],
  unda:["印欧 wed-(水)的鼻化形——英语 water、俄语 voda(伏特加 vodka=\"小水\"!)全是亲戚。unda 专指\"动起来的水\"。","Nasalized form of Indo-European wed- (water) — kin to water and Russian voda (vodka = \"little water\"!). Unda is water in motion."],
  tempestas:["tempus(时间)加状态词尾:本义只是\"一段天时\",好坏皆可。海上的\"天时\"多半凶险,词义便沉向了风暴。","tempus (time) plus a state-ending: originally just \"a spell of weather,\" good or bad. At sea the weather mostly turned foul — so did the word."],
  portus:["印欧 per-(穿过、渡过):港口是\"渡口\",大门是 porta,搬运是 portare——同一个\"通过\"生出一族词。","Indo-European per- (to pass through): a port is a crossing-place; porta the gate and portare to carry grew from the same passage."],
  terra:["源自 ters-(干):terra 本义\"干的地方\"——与海相对的干燥陆地。英语 thirst(渴)是同根的日耳曼亲戚:干,就渴。","From ters- (dry): terra is \"the dry place,\" land as opposed to sea. English thirst is Germanic kin: what is dry, thirsts."],
  nubes:["印欧 sneudh-(雾、遮盖):云是\"遮住天的东西\"。罗马占卜官最怕它——云一来,神的征兆就看不见了。","Indo-European sneudh- (mist, cover): a cloud is what veils the sky. Roman augurs dreaded it — clouds hid the omens of the gods."],
  pluvia:["动词 pluere(下雨)的名词。罗马人祈雨向朱庇特喊 Iuppiter Pluvius——\"降雨的天父\"。","Noun of pluere (to rain). Praying for rain, Romans called on Iuppiter Pluvius — \"Jupiter who rains.\""],
  remus:["印欧 ere-(划动)加工具词尾:划水的器具。与罗马建城者 Remus 同形不同源——纯属巧合。","Indo-European ere- (to row) with a tool-ending: the rowing thing. Identical to the name of Rome's Remus by pure coincidence."],
  velum:["或从 vehere(运载)来:帆是\"载船前行的布\"。同一块布挂在脸上就是面纱——遮风与遮面,一词两用。","Perhaps from vehere (to carry): the sail is \"the cloth that carries the ship.\" Hung on a face, the same cloth is a veil."],
  piscis:["古欧洲的\"鱼\"字:拉丁 piscis、英语 fish、爱尔兰语 iasc 同根。基督教早期用鱼作暗号,这个词游进了信仰史。","Old Europe's fish-word: Latin piscis, English fish, Irish iasc share the root. Early Christians used the fish as a secret sign — the word swam into faith."],
  iter:["动词 ire(走)的名词:\"走过的路程\"。罗马军团一天的行军也叫 iter——他们用脚把这个词踩遍了欧洲。","Noun of ire (to go): the distance walked. A legion's daily march was an iter — Roman feet stamped this word across Europe."],
  /* ---- C1 L3 ---- */
  porta:["per-(穿过)家族:城墙上\"可以穿过的地方\"。罗马建城时用犁划界,抬犁留出的缺口就是 porta——门是被神圣豁免的墙。","Of the per- (pass through) family: the place in a wall you may pass. Founding a city, Romans ploughed its boundary and lifted the plough where gates would be — a porta is a hallowed gap."],
  ianua:["以双面门神 Ianus(雅努斯)命名:他一张脸朝里一张脸朝外,守着每一道门与每一个开端——包括一年的开端一月。","Named for two-faced Ianus, who looks in and out at once, guarding every door and every beginning — including the year's own door, January."],
  murus:["古拉丁 moiros,本义\"围起来的\"。罗马传说里,雷穆斯嘲笑罗慕路斯的新墙太矮而跳了过去——为此付出了生命:墙,不可轻慢。","Old Latin moiros, \"that which encloses.\" In legend Remus mocked Romulus's low new wall by leaping it — and died for it: walls were sacred."],
  turris:["经希腊 tyrris 传入,可能源自地中海更古老的民族——塔楼比拉丁语更早矗立在爱琴海岸。","Via Greek tyrris, likely from a pre-Greek Mediterranean people — towers stood on Aegean shores before Latin had a word for them."],
  aula:["希腊 aule 本指\"庭院\"——荷马笔下国王在庭院里设宴。词随建筑长大,从院子变成王家大厅。","Greek aule meant courtyard — Homer's kings feasted there. As palaces grew, the word moved indoors to mean the great hall."],
  mensa:["或为 metiri(测量)的分词:\"量好的(食物份)\"→摆食物的桌子。罗马预言说\"饿到吃桌子\"——埃涅阿斯用面饼当盘子吃掉,预言应验,罗马落址。","Perhaps from metiri (to measure): \"the measured portion\" → the board it sat on. A prophecy said Aeneas would be hungry enough to eat his tables — he ate his bread-plates, and Rome found its home."],
  sella:["sed-(坐)加工具词尾,sedla 说快了变 sella。罗马高官的象牙折椅 sella curulis 是权力本身——椅子在哪,法庭就在哪。","sed- (sit) with a tool-ending; sedla said fast became sella. A magistrate's ivory folding chair, the sella curulis, WAS his power: where the chair stood, court was in session."],
  lucerna:["lux(光)加器具词尾:\"造光的器物\"。庞贝出土的陶油灯上还留着灯芯的焦痕——两千年前的夜读就靠它。","lux (light) plus a tool-ending: \"the light-maker.\" Clay lamps dug from Pompeii still carry scorched wicks — two-thousand-year-old late-night reading."],
  fenestra:["可能借自伊特鲁里亚人——罗马人的老师民族。墙上开洞需要技术,连\"窗\"这个词都是进口的。","Probably borrowed from the Etruscans, Rome's teacher-nation. Cutting holes in walls took skill — even the word for window was imported."],
  tectum:["动词 tegere(覆盖)的分词:\"被盖上的\"=屋顶。印欧词根 steg- 同时生出希腊 stegos 和英语 thatch(茅草顶)。","Participle of tegere (to cover): \"the covered thing\" = roof. Root steg- also gave Greek stegos and English thatch."],
  columna:["词根 cel-(高耸)家族,与 collis(山丘)、culmen(顶点)同源:柱子是\"人造的高处\"。","Family of cel- (to rise): kin to collis (hill) and culmen (summit). A column is a man-made height."],
  statua:["stare(站)→ statuere(立起)→ statua:被立起来的像。罗马广场挤满名人雕像,失宠者的像会被推倒回炉——连石头也有政治。","stare (stand) → statuere (set up) → statua: the thing set standing. The Forum bristled with statues; a disgraced man's was toppled and melted — even stone had politics."],
  hortus:["印欧 gher-(围起):菜园就是\"围起来的地\"。英语 garden、yard 同根——花园的本质是一圈篱笆。","Indo-European gher- (to enclose): a garden is \"the fenced ground.\" English garden and yard share the root — a garden is, at heart, its fence."],
  fons:["或与 fundere(涌出)相关:泉是\"大地涌出的东西\"。罗马每口泉都住着一位泉神,至今罗马街头仍有千座喷泉在流这份古老敬意。","Perhaps kin to fundere (to pour forth): a spring is what the earth pours out. Every Roman spring housed a spirit — a thousand fountains still pour that reverence today."],
  aurum:["或与 aurora(曙光)同根:金子是\"曙光色的金属\"。化学符号 Au 就是这缕晨光的缩写。","Perhaps kin to aurora (dawn): gold is the dawn-colored metal. The symbol Au abbreviates that morning light."],
  argentum:["印欧 arg-(发白光):银是\"白亮的金属\"。一整个国家以它命名——阿根廷,白银之国。","Indo-European arg- (to shine white): silver is the white-gleaming metal. A whole nation is named for it — Argentina, land of silver."],
  marmor:["希腊 marmaros(闪亮的石头),与 marmairein(闪烁)同族。雅典卫城的大理石在阳光下真的会闪——词根说的是实话。","Greek marmaros, kin to marmairein (to flash). Acropolis marble truly glitters in the sun — the root tells the plain truth."],
  pictura:["pingere(画)本义\"用针刺、绣\"——最早的\"画\"是刺绣和纹身。词根 peig- 也生出英语 paint。","pingere (to paint) first meant to prick or embroider — the earliest \"painting\" was needlework and tattoo. Root peig- also gave paint."],
  servus:["或从\"看守羊群者\"而来。罗马奴隶可赎身成自由民,奥地利问候语 Servus!(愿为您效劳)让这个词至今每天被说出口。","Perhaps from \"the flock-watcher.\" Roman slaves could buy freedom; Austria's greeting Servus! (\"at your service\") keeps the word on daily lips."],
  convivium:["con(一起)+ vivere(活着):宴会是\"一起活着\"。罗马人认为独食非人——吃饭的意义一半在同桌。","con (together) + vivere (to live): a banquet is \"living together.\" Romans thought eating alone barely human — half of every meal is the company."],
  /* ---- C1 L4 ---- */
  filum:["印欧 gwhi-(线、筋):从纺锤上抽出的那一缕。命运三女神纺的也是 filum——人的一生就是一根线的长度。","Indo-European gwhi- (thread, sinew): the strand drawn from the spindle. The Fates spin filum too — a life is the length of one thread."],
  gladius:["可能借自凯尔特语——罗马人向高卢对手学了短剑,连剑的名字一起缴获。","Likely a Celtic loan — Rome learned the short sword from Gaulish foes and captured its name along with it."],
  donum:["印欧 do-(给)加成果词尾:被给出的东西。梵语 dana(布施)、俄语 dar 同根——\"给予\"是最早全球化的动词之一。","Indo-European do- (give) plus a result-ending: the thing given. Sanskrit dana and Russian dar share it — giving was among the first global verbs."],
  corona:["希腊 korone(弯曲物→花环)。罗马最高军功章是一顶草环 corona graminea——由被救的军团亲手编成,比黄金贵重。","Greek korone (curved thing → garland). Rome's highest medal was a grass crown, corona graminea, woven by the very legion a man had saved — worth more than gold."],
  anulus:["anus(圆环)加小词尾:\"小环\"。罗马人把图章戒指当身份证,骑士阶层的金戒指就是阶级本身。","anus (ring) with a diminutive: \"little circle.\" A Roman's signet ring was his ID; the knight's gold ring was his very rank."],
  gemma:["本义\"植物的芽\"。罗马人觉得宝石就是大地冒出的芽苞——地里长出的春天,凝固成了石头。","Originally a plant's bud. Romans saw gems as the earth's own budding — springtime pushed up from below and frozen into stone."],
  arca:["arcere(关住、挡住)的产物:能锁住东西的柜子。《圣经》拉丁译本用它译挪亚方舟和约柜——装着全世界的箱子。","From arcere (to shut in): the chest that locks things away. The Latin Bible used it for Noah's Ark and the Ark of the Covenant — boxes holding the world."],
  clavis:["印欧 klau-(钩、闩):最早的钥匙就是拨门闩的弯钩。希腊 kleis 同根——钩子开门,概念四千年未变。","Indo-European klau- (hook, bar): the first key was a bent hook that lifted the bolt. Greek kleis is the same — a hook opening a door, unchanged in four millennia."],
  charta:["希腊 chartes:尼罗河纸莎草压成的书页。纸的技术从埃及经希腊到罗马,词也跟着旅行——纸未变,charta 已行千里。","Greek chartes: a leaf of pressed Nile papyrus. The craft travelled from Egypt through Greece to Rome, and the word travelled with the paper."],
  epistula:["希腊 epistole,来自 epistellein(派送):信是\"被派出去的话\"。罗马大道每隔几十里设驿站,epistula 沿着帝国血管流动。","Greek epistole, from epistellein (to send forth): a letter is speech dispatched. Relay posts lined the Roman roads — epistulae flowed through the empire's veins."],
  liber:["本义\"树的内皮\"——罗马人最早在树皮上写字。后来纸换了,名字没换:每本书里都藏着一棵树。","Originally the inner bark of a tree — Rome's first writing surface. The material changed, the name stayed: every book still hides a tree."],
  verbum:["印欧 were-(说,郑重地讲):英语 word 是它的日耳曼兄弟。《圣经·约翰福音》开篇的\"太初有道\",拉丁文正是 Verbum。","Indo-European were- (to speak solemnly): English word is its Germanic brother. \"In the beginning was the Word\" — in Latin, the Verbum."],
  consilium:["con(共同)+ 商议动词 consulere:一群人坐下来一起想。罗马元老院的日常就是 consilium——共和国靠开会运转。","con (together) + consulere (to deliberate): people sitting down to think as one. The Senate's daily work was consilium — the Republic ran on meetings."],
  fides:["印欧 bheidh-(信任、托付)。罗马有 Fides 女神,神庙里签条约、存档案——国家的承诺放在\"信任\"的屋檐下。","Indo-European bheidh- (to trust, entrust). Rome kept a goddess Fides; treaties were signed and archived in her temple — the state's word lived under Trust's roof."],
  amor:["amare(爱)的名词,词根或来自婴儿语 am-(如\"妈妈\"的呢喃)。罗马城 Roma 倒着拼是 Amor——诗人们玩了两千年这个秘密。","Noun of amare, perhaps from nursery babble am-. Spell Roma backwards and you get Amor — poets have played with that secret for two thousand years."],
  cor:["印欧 kerd-(心):希腊 kardia、英语 heart 同根。古人认为心是思想与勇气的居所——所以\"记在心里\"\"鼓起心气\"全落在这个词上。","Indo-European kerd- (heart): Greek kardia and English heart are kin. The ancients housed thought and courage in the heart — so remembering and daring both landed on this word."],
  animus:["印欧 ane-(呼吸):希腊 anemos 是\"风\"。呼吸=生命=心灵——古人从一口气里看见了灵魂。","Indo-European ane- (to breathe): Greek anemos means wind. Breath = life = mind — the ancients found the soul inside a single breath."],
  sapientia:["sapere 本义\"有味道\",引申\"能品出味道\"→有判断力。智慧在拉丁语里是一种味觉:懂生活,先会尝。","sapere first meant \"to have flavor,\" then \"to taste well\" → to judge well. Wisdom in Latin is a kind of palate: to know life, first learn to taste it."],
  secretum:["se(分开)+ cernere(筛):筛到一边去的东西。秘密的本义是\"被单独放置\"——不给所有人看的那一堆。","se (apart) + cernere (to sift): what is sifted aside. A secret is literally \"the set-apart pile\" — not for every eye."],
  auxilium:["augere(增大)家族:援助就是\"给你增力\"。罗马军团的盟军部队就叫 auxilia——正规军之外加上来的力量。","Family of augere (to increase): help is strength added to yours. Rome's allied troops were the auxilia — force added beyond the legions."],
  /* ---- C1 L5 ---- */
  lux:["印欧 leuk-(发光)——人类最亮的词根之一:希腊 leukos(白)、英语 light、拉丁 lux 同源。罗马人发誓爱说\"以这光起誓\"。","Indo-European leuk- (to shine) — one of the brightest roots: Greek leukos, English light, Latin lux. Romans swore oaths \"by this light.\""],
  tenebrae:["永远用复数——黑暗在拉丁语里不是\"一个\",是包围你的\"一群\"。词根或与\"浓稠\"有关:暗是稠得化不开的东西。","Always plural — darkness in Latin is not one thing but a crowd around you. The root may mean \"thick\": dark is what will not thin."],
  via:["印欧 wegh-(移动、运送):英语 way、wagon 同根。罗马大道让 via 成为文明词——条条大路通罗马,说的就是它。","Indo-European wegh- (to move, convey): kin to way and wagon. Rome's highways made via a word of civilization — all roads lead to Rome, and each one is a via."],
  umbra:["词源幽暗如其义。罗马人把死者的魂也叫 umbrae——影子:人死后剩下的,是他的影。","Its origin is as shadowy as its meaning. Romans called the dead umbrae — shades: what remains of a man is his shadow."],
  silentium:["silere(无声)的名词。罗马宗教仪式开场先喊 favete linguis——\"管住舌头\":与神对话,先学会沉默。","Noun of silere (to be still). Roman rites opened with favete linguis — \"hold your tongues\": to speak with gods, first master silence."],
  timor:["timere(怕)的名词。罗马军营里立过 Timor 的祭坛——他们连恐惧都要祭拜:承认它,才能指挥它。","Noun of timere (to fear). Roman camps raised altars to Timor himself — they worshipped even fear: admit it, and you can command it."],
  ignis:["印欧 egni-(火):梵语的火神就叫 Agni。罗马灶神庙里的圣火由维斯塔贞女守护,千年不灭——ignis 是罗马的心跳。","Indo-European egni-: the Vedic fire-god IS Agni. Vesta's sacred flame, tended by her virgins, burned for a thousand years — ignis was Rome's heartbeat."],
  fax:["罗马婚礼由火把队伍护送新娘回家,葬礼由火把引路下葬——fax 照亮人生的两端。","Torch processions led the bride home and the dead to rest — the fax lit both ends of a Roman life."],
  flamma:["flagrare(熊熊燃烧)家族,flag-ma 缩成 flamma:火焰是\"燃烧这个动作本身的形状\"。","Family of flagrare (to blaze); flag-ma contracted to flamma: a flame is the very shape of burning."],
  saxum:["或与 secare(切割)同根:岩石是\"能崩裂出碎片的东西\"——罗马人从石头看到的第一件事是它能被开采。","Perhaps kin to secare (to cut): rock is \"that which breaks into pieces\" — the first thing Romans saw in stone was the quarry."],
  labyrinthus:["前希腊语古词,或与 labrys(双面斧)有关——克诺索斯宫殿满墙双斧标记,\"迷宫\"可能就是\"双斧之宫\"。","A pre-Greek word, perhaps from labrys (double-axe) — Knossos is covered in double-axe marks; the Labyrinth may be \"the House of the Double Axe.\""],
  angulus:["印欧 ang-(弯曲):角落是\"空间打弯的地方\"。英语 ankle(脚踝)同根——身体的小角落。","Indo-European ang- (to bend): a corner is where space bends. English ankle shares the root — the body's own little corner."],
  dexter:["印欧 deks-(右):多数人右手更巧,\"右\"在各语言里都长成了\"灵巧、吉利\"。罗马占卜以右为吉——词根里藏着偏心。","Indo-European deks- (right): most hands are defter on the right, so \"right\" grew to mean skillful and lucky everywhere. Roman augury favored the right — the root plays favorites."],
  sinister:["本义只是\"左边\"。罗马占卜官面南观鸟,左侧凶兆多——于是\"左\"慢慢变成\"不祥\"。一个词的堕落史,全怪几只鸟。","It meant simply \"left.\" Facing south, Roman augurs read ill omens on that side — so \"left\" slid into \"ominous.\" A word's whole downfall, blamed on birds."],
  ante:["印欧 ant-(额头、面前):在前=在你的脸面对的方向。希腊 anti(对面→反对)是同根兄弟——面前站久了就成了对手。","Indo-European ant- (forehead, front): before = where your face points. Greek anti (facing → against) is its brother — stand in front long enough and you become the opponent."],
  post:["古拉丁 posti,\"在后、在……之后\"。罗马人刻在墓碑上的 post mortem(死后)让它成为最沉静的介词。","Old Latin posti, \"behind, after.\" Carved on tombstones in post mortem, it became the quietest of prepositions."],
  sub:["印欧 upo(自下而上)的拉丁形——同一词根在英语里成了 up!sub 说\"在下\",up 说\"向上\",本是一家。","Latin shape of Indo-European upo (from below) — the same root became English up! sub says \"under,\" up says \"upward\": one family."],
  super:["upo 加比较级成 uper→super:\"更上面\"。希腊 hyper 是孪生兄弟——super 与 hyper,罗马与希腊的同一个\"之上\"。","upo with a comparative became uper → super: \"higher above.\" Greek hyper is its twin — Rome's and Greece's same \"above.\""],
  intra:["in(在内)的方位扩展形:\"在……范围之内\"。罗马城的神圣边界 pomerium 之内叫 intra pomerium——武装不得入内,词里带着城墙。","Extended form of in: \"within the bounds of.\" Inside Rome's sacred line was intra pomerium — no armed men allowed; the word carries the wall."],
  exitus:["ex(出)+ ire(走)的分词:\"走出去(的地方)\"。罗马剧场散场的门、人生的终点、故事的结局——拉丁语全用它。","ex (out) + ire (go), as a noun: the going-out (place). Theatre doors, life's end, a story's close — Latin used exitus for them all."],
  /* ---- C1 L6 ---- */
  vox:["印欧 wekw-(说出):希腊 epos(史诗)竟是同根——嗓音与史诗,同一次发声。","Indo-European wekw- (to utter): Greek epos (epic) is the same root — a voice and an epic, one act of speaking."],
  auris:["印欧 ous-(耳):希腊 ous、英语 ear 同源。三千年里人类换了无数语言,耳朵的名字几乎没变形。","Indo-European ous- (ear): Greek ous and English ear are kin. Languages came and went; the ear barely changed its name."],
  oculus:["印欧 okw-(看):希腊 ops(眼)、ophthalmos 同根。奥德修斯刺瞎的独眼巨人 Cyclops=\"圆眼\"——同一个词根。","Indo-European okw- (to see): Greek ops and ophthalmos share it. The Cyclops Odysseus blinded is \"Round-Eye\" — the very same root."],
  manus:["古意大利词:手。罗马新娘行 in manum 礼——把自己交到丈夫\"手\"里;释放奴隶叫 manumissio——\"从手里放出去\"。手在罗马法里就是权力。","An old Italic word. A bride passed in manum — into her husband's hand; freeing a slave was manumissio, \"sending from the hand.\" In Roman law the hand WAS power."],
  pes:["印欧 ped-(脚):希腊 pous、英语 foot 同根。罗马人用脚量世界:一步 passus,千步 mille passus=一英里。","Indo-European ped- (foot): Greek pous, English foot. Romans measured the world by feet: one passus a pace, a thousand of them a mile."],
  caput:["印欧 kaput-(头):英语 head 是同根远亲。罗马把\"人头\"当法律单位——丧失公民权叫 capitis deminutio,\"头的减损\"。","Indo-European kaput-: English head is distant kin. Rome made the head a legal unit — losing citizenship was capitis deminutio, \"a lessening of the head.\""],
  corpus:["印欧 kwrep-(形体)。罗马法把\"多人合成一体\"称为 corpus——公司、社团、文集:凡是把散的合成整的,都借这具\"身体\"。","Indo-European kwrep- (form, body). Roman law called any many-made-one a corpus — guilds, colleges, collected works: whatever binds the scattered into a whole borrows this body."],
  os:["印欧 os-(嘴):梵语 as- 同根。拉丁语用它表达\"当面\"——coram ore,话从嘴出,脸就是证词。","Indo-European os- (mouth): Sanskrit as- is kin. Latin used it for \"face to face\" — words leave the mouth, and the face testifies."],
  nasus:["印欧 nas-(鼻):英语 nose、俄语 nos 同根。罗马人说\"有鼻子\"(nasutus)意为机敏毒舌——鼻子是嗅出愚蠢的器官。","Indo-European nas-: English nose, Russian nos. To \"have a nose\" (nasutus) meant wit and sarcasm — the organ that sniffs out folly."],
  digitus:["或与 dicere(指出)同根:手指是\"用来指的东西\"。古人掰指计数,于是手指顺便成了\"数字\"。","Perhaps kin to dicere (to point out): fingers are the pointing things. People counted on them, so fingers became digits."],
  bracchium:["希腊 brachion(上臂),本义\"较短的\"——相对前臂而言。罗马工程师用它称呼一切伸出的支臂:词从身体长进机械。","Greek brachion (upper arm), literally \"the shorter one.\" Roman engineers used it for any projecting arm — the word grew from body into machine."],
  genu:["印欧 genu-(膝):希腊 gony、英语 knee 同根。古人认膝盖为生命力所在,求饶必抱膝——屈膝是最古老的外交。","Indo-European genu-: Greek gony, English knee. The knee held life-force; suppliants clasped it — kneeling is diplomacy's oldest move."],
  capillus:["或从 caput(头)来:\"头上的东西\"。罗马贵妇发型高耸如塔,理发师 tonsor 是城里最忙的手艺人。","Perhaps from caput: \"what the head wears.\" Roman ladies piled towering coiffures; the tonsor was the busiest craftsman in town."],
  dens:["印欧 dent-(咬的东西→牙),edere(吃)的古分词:牙就是\"正在吃的\"。英语 tooth 同根异路。","Indo-European dent-, an old participle of \"to eat\": teeth are \"the eating ones.\" English tooth took the Germanic road."],
  lingua:["古拉丁 dingua,受 lingere(舔)影响改成 l——舌头是\"舔的器官\"。一个器官管说话,于是\"舌\"顺势成了\"语言\"。","Old Latin dingua, reshaped by lingere (to lick) — the licking organ. Since it also speaks, \"tongue\" slid naturally into \"language.\""],
  sanguis:["专指\"流动的活血\"——凝固的血另叫 cruor。罗马人在血里看见生命与家系:bloodline 的观念,拉丁语早已备好词。","Living, flowing blood — clotted blood had its own word, cruor. Romans read life and lineage in it: the idea of a bloodline was ready-made in Latin."],
  spiritus:["spirare(呼吸)是拟声词根——双唇吐气的 sp- 声。古人看见:活人有气,死人无气。于是\"一口气\"渐渐升华成\"灵魂与精神\"——神把气吹进泥土,人才活了。","spirare (to breathe) is echoic — the sp- of lips releasing air. The living breathe, the dead do not: so \"a breath\" rose to mean soul and spirit — god breathed into clay, and it lived."],
  somnus:["印欧 swep-(睡):希腊 hypnos 是同根兄弟。罗马神话里睡神 Somnus 与死神是孪生子——古人早看出睡与死只隔一层醒。","Indo-European swep- (sleep): Greek hypnos is its brother. In myth Somnus and Death are twins — the ancients knew sleep and death differ by one waking."],
  vita:["vivere(活)的名词,印欧 gwei-(活着)家族——希腊 bios、zoe 同根。罗马墓志铭爱写 dum vivimus vivamus:活着,就好好活。","Noun of vivere, family of Indo-European gwei- (to live) — Greek bios and zoe are kin. Roman epitaphs urged dum vivimus vivamus: while we live, let us LIVE."],
  mors:["印欧 mer-(死):梵语 mrtyu、俄语 smert 同根。罗马凯旋式上,奴隶在将军耳边反复低语 memento mori——记住你会死。","Indo-European mer- (to die): Sanskrit mrtyu, Russian smert. At a triumph a slave whispered in the general's ear, memento mori — remember you will die."],
  /* ---- C1 L7 ---- */
  taurus:["地中海古词,或经腓尼基传入——克里特的跳牛壁画、米诺陶、金牛座:公牛是爱琴文明的图腾,词比拉丁语更老。","An old Mediterranean word, perhaps via Phoenician — bull-leaping frescoes, the Minotaur, Taurus: the bull was the Aegean's totem, and the word is older than Latin."],
  leo:["经希腊 leon 传入,源头或在埃及、闪米特世界——欧洲无狮,词随着旅行者的惊叹进口。","Via Greek leon, ultimately from Egypt or the Semitic world — Europe had no lions; the word arrived with travellers' awe."],
  lupus:["印欧 wlkwo-(狼)的拉丁变形,英语 wolf 同根。母狼哺育了罗慕路斯与雷穆斯——罗马是狼奶喂大的城。","Latin shape of Indo-European wlkwo-; English wolf is kin. A she-wolf nursed Romulus and Remus — Rome was raised on wolf's milk."],
  serpens:["serpere(爬行)的分词:\"正在爬的东西\"。拉丁语不敢直呼蛇名,用动作代替——这是委婉语:说\"爬行者\",别把它唤来。","Participle of serpere (to creep): \"the creeping one.\" Latin avoids naming the snake, using its motion instead — a taboo word: say \"creeper,\" lest you summon it."],
  aquila:["或从 aquilus(深暗色)来:鹰是\"深色大鸟\"。罗马军团的银鹰旗就叫 aquila,丢鹰即军团之耻——为夺回一面鹰旗可以再打一场战争。","Perhaps from aquilus (dark-hued): the dark great bird. The legion's silver standard WAS the aquila; losing it disgraced the legion — wars were fought to win an eagle back."],
  equus:["印欧 ekwo-(马):梵语 asva、希腊 hippos 同根。马随印欧人征服欧亚,这个词跟着马蹄跑了五千年。","Indo-European ekwo-: Sanskrit asva, Greek hippos. The horse carried the Indo-Europeans across Eurasia, and the word ran with its hooves for five thousand years."],
  canis:["印欧 kwon-(狗):希腊 kyon、英语 hound 同根。庞贝人家门口的马赛克写着 cave canem——小心恶犬:两千年前的\"内有恶犬\"。","Indo-European kwon-: Greek kyon, English hound. Pompeii doorways warn cave canem — beware of the dog: a two-thousand-year-old sign."],
  felis:["词源不明——猫本身也是晚客,从埃及经希腊才走进罗马家庭。神秘的动物,配一个来历不明的名字。","Origin unknown — the cat itself came late, from Egypt via Greece into Roman homes. A mysterious animal deserves a word with no traceable past."],
  avis:["印欧 awi-(鸟):梵语 vi- 同根。罗马占卜核心就是观鸟(auspicium=avis+specere\"看鸟\")——国家大事,先问飞鸟。","Indo-European awi-: Sanskrit vi- is kin. Roman augury WAS bird-watching — auspicium = avis + specere, \"bird-viewing\": affairs of state began by asking the birds."],
  ursus:["印欧 rktos(熊)的拉丁后代,希腊 arktos 同根——北极 Arctic 就是\"熊之地\"(大熊座所指的北方)。","Latin child of Indo-European rktos; Greek arktos is kin — the Arctic is \"bear-land,\" the north beneath the Great Bear."],
  aper:["古欧洲猎人的词,德语 Eber 同根。史诗里野猪是英雄的试炼——卡吕冬野猪狩猎集合了全希腊的豪杰。","An old European hunters' word; German Eber is kin. In epic the boar tested heroes — the Calydonian Hunt gathered all of Greece's best."],
  cervus:["印欧 ker-(角):鹿是\"长角的\"。同根的 cornu(角)、英语 horn、hart(牡鹿)——一个词根顶着一对角。","Indo-European ker- (horn): the deer is \"the horned one.\" Kin: cornu, English horn and hart — one root wearing antlers."],
  vacca:["古意大利词,或与梵语 vasa(母牛)遥亲。1796 年牛痘救了人类,疫苗 vaccine 从此把\"母牛\"写进了医学史。","An old Italic word, perhaps distant kin of Sanskrit vasa. In 1796 cowpox saved mankind — vaccine wrote \"the cow\" into medical history forever."],
  ovis:["印欧 owi-(羊):梵语 avi、英语 ewe 同根。牧羊是印欧人的老本行,这个词五千年没换过牧场。","Indo-European owi-: Sanskrit avi, English ewe. Shepherding was the Indo-Europeans' oldest trade; the word never changed pastures in five millennia."],
  capra:["与 caper(公山羊)一族,或源于\"跳跃\"——山羊是\"蹦跳的家伙\"。意大利卡普里岛 Capri 可能就是\"山羊岛\"。","Family of caper (he-goat), perhaps from \"to leap\" — the goat is the bounder. The isle of Capri may simply be Goat Island."],
  porcus:["印欧 porko-(小猪):英语 farrow(猪崽)、俄语 porosenok 同根。农家词根,五千年一直在圈里。","Indo-European porko- (young pig): kin to farrow and Russian porosenok. A farmyard root, penned in for five thousand years."],
  mus:["印欧 mus(鼠):梵语、希腊、拉丁、英语 mouse 全同形。肌肉 musculus=\"小老鼠\"——古人看二头肌滚动,像皮下窜过一只鼠。","Indo-European mus: identical in Sanskrit, Greek, Latin and English mouse. A muscle is musculus, \"little mouse\" — a flexing biceps scurries under the skin."],
  rana:["拟声词:罗马人听蛙鸣就是 ra-na ra-na。青蛙自己报的名,人类只负责记下来。","Onomatopoeia: to Roman ears the croak said ra-na, ra-na. The frog named itself; humans merely wrote it down."],
  apis:["或借自埃及——尼罗河圣牛也叫 Apis,而埃及正是古代养蜂圣地。蜜蜂与神牛共享一个进口名字。","Perhaps from Egypt — the sacred Nile bull was also Apis, and Egypt was antiquity's great beekeeping land. Bee and holy bull share one imported name."],
  monstrum:["monere(警示)+ 工具词尾:怪物原是\"神发出的警告\"——畸形与异象都是上天的短信。看见怪物不是恐怖片,是神谕。","monere (to warn) plus a tool-ending: a monster was heaven's warning — prodigies and freaks were messages from above. Seeing one wasn't horror; it was oracle."],
  /* ---- C1 L8 ---- */
  pugna:["pugnus(拳头)的战斗:最早的战斗就是挥拳。印欧 peuk-(刺、戳)——从拳头到长矛,词根一路升级装备。","Battle from pugnus (fist): the first fight was fists. Root peuk- (to pierce) — from knuckles to spears, the root kept upgrading its weapons."],
  bellum:["古拉丁 duellum(二人相争)缩成 bellum——决斗放大成战争。duel 保留了老形:两个词,一场架的两个规模。","Old Latin duellum (strife of two) contracted to bellum — the duel scaled up to war. Duel keeps the old shape: one quarrel, two sizes."],
  hasta:["古意大利长矛。罗马拍卖时把矛插在地上作官方标志(sub hasta\"矛下出售\")——西语 subasta(拍卖)至今扛着这根矛。","The old Italic spear. Roman auctions planted one in the ground as the official sign — sold sub hasta, \"under the spear\"; Spanish subasta still carries it."],
  scutum:["印欧 skei-(切、劈)家族:盾最早是劈出来的木板。罗马大盾能组成\"龟甲阵\"testudo——一面盾是木板,千面盾是城墙。","Family of skei- (to split): the first shield was a hewn plank. Locked together, Roman scuta formed the testudo — one shield is a board, a thousand are a wall."],
  galea:["或借自希腊 galee(鼬)——早期头盔用鼬皮或以鼬为饰。一顶帽子里藏着一只小兽。","Perhaps from Greek galee (weasel) — early helmets used its pelt or crest. A small beast hides inside the helmet."],
  arma:["印欧 ar-(接合、装配):武器是\"装配到身上的东西\"。同根的 ars(技艺)提醒我们:兵器与艺术,都是\"装配\"出来的。","Indo-European ar- (to fit together): arms are what you fit onto yourself. Kin ars (art) reminds us — weapons and art are both things well-fitted."],
  sagitta:["词源不明,可能借自某个消失的古地中海民族——箭比拉丁人更早飞过意大利的天空。","Origin unknown — likely borrowed from some vanished Mediterranean people: arrows flew over Italy before Latin arrived."],
  arcus:["本义\"弓\",引申\"弓形\"——彩虹、拱门、凯旋门全是 arcus。罗马人把胜利建成一张石头的弓。","First the bow, then anything bow-shaped — rainbow, archway, triumphal arch. Rome built victory as a bow of stone."],
  hostis:["最初指\"陌生人、客人\"——与英语 guest 同根!城邦时代陌生人渐成威胁,词义从\"客\"滑向\"敌\"。一词记录了信任的崩塌。","First \"stranger, guest\" — same root as guest! As city-states hardened, the stranger became the threat: one word records the collapse of trust."],
  miles:["或从 mille(千)来:军团千人为伍,士兵就是\"千人队里的一个\"。个体消失在编制里——名字都属于队列。","Perhaps from mille (thousand): the legion counted by thousands, a soldier was \"one of the thousand.\" The individual dissolved into the roster — even his name belonged to the column."],
  fortis:["古拉丁 forctis,或与 bhergh-(高、强)相关。罗马格言 fortes fortuna adiuvat——命运女神偏爱勇者。","Old Latin forctis, perhaps from bhergh- (high, strong). The proverb: fortes fortuna adiuvat — fortune favors the brave."],
  magnus:["印欧 meg-(大):希腊 megas、梵语 maha-(摩诃!)同根。亚历山大、查理曼、庞培都挂过 Magnus——\"大\"是史上最抢手的头衔。","Indo-European meg-: Greek megas, Sanskrit maha-. Alexander, Charlemagne and Pompey all wore Magnus — \"the Great\" is history's most contested title."],
  parvus:["印欧 pau-(少、小):希腊 pais(孩子)、英语 few 同根。小与少与幼,一个词根三副面孔。","Indo-European pau- (little, few): Greek pais (child) and English few are kin. Small, few and young — three faces of one root."],
  celer:["或与希腊 keles(快马)同根:快,最早是马的品质。罗马最早的骑兵就叫 celeres——\"快者们\"。","Perhaps kin to Greek keles (racehorse): speed was first a horse's virtue. Rome's earliest cavalry were the celeres — \"the swift ones.\""],
  tardus:["词源不明——迟缓这个词自己就走得慢,来历早被落在路上。罗马诗人用它形容冬日、老年与犹豫。","Origin unknown — slowness travels slowly, and its past got left on the road. Poets used it for winter, old age and hesitation."],
  victoria:["vincere(战胜)的成果名词。罗马元老院开会的祭坛上立着胜利女神 Victoria 的金像——开会先拜胜利。","Result-noun of vincere (to conquer). A golden Victoria stood on the Senate's altar — every session began by saluting Victory."],
  gloria:["词源不明,义为\"名声的光辉\"。罗马人视 gloria 为人生目标——但也警告:sic transit gloria mundi,世间荣耀转眼即逝。","Origin unknown; \"the shining of renown.\" Romans lived for gloria — yet warned: sic transit gloria mundi, so passes the glory of the world."],
  virtus:["vir(男子汉)+ -tus:字面\"男子气概\",在罗马涵盖勇敢、正直、担当——后被哲学家扩为\"美德\":做人的全部功课。","vir (man) + -tus: literally manliness — courage, integrity, duty; philosophers widened it to virtue: the whole curriculum of being human."],
  ferrum:["或借自闪米特语,随铁器贸易传入——铁本身就是进口货,名字随船到岸。化学符号 Fe 让它永远留在元素周期表。","Perhaps Semitic, arriving with the iron trade — the metal was an import, and its name came ashore with the cargo. The symbol Fe keeps it on the periodic table forever."],
  clamor:["clamare(呼喊)的名词,印欧 kele-(喊)家族——英语 low(牛叫)竟是远亲。战场、剧场、市场:罗马的喧嚣全装在这个词里。","Noun of clamare, family of kele- (to shout) — English \"low\" (a cow's call) is distant kin. Battlefield, theatre, market: all Rome's din fits in this word."],
  /* ---- C1 L9 ---- */
  unus:["印欧 oi-no-(一):英语 one、德语 ein 同根。骰子的幺点古英语叫 ace——追根也是这个\"一\"。","Indo-European oi-no-: English one, German ein. The ace on a die traces to the same \"one.\""],
  duo:["印欧 dwo-(二):英语 two、梵语 dva 同根。二是最早的\"对立\":双数、双方、二心——分歧从数字二开始。","Indo-European dwo-: English two, Sanskrit dva. Two is the first opposition — both sides, double minds: disagreement begins at this number."],
  tres:["印欧 trei-(三):英语 three、梵语 tri 同根。古人视三为\"完整\":开始、中间、结束——所以祈祷喊三遍。","Indo-European trei-: English three, Sanskrit tri. Three felt complete — beginning, middle, end — so prayers were said thrice."],
  quattuor:["印欧 kwetwor-(四):英语 four 同根异形。罗马数字 IV 曾写作 IIII——钟表盘上至今保留这古老写法。","Indo-European kwetwor-: four is the same root reshaped. Roman IV was once IIII — clock faces keep the old spelling still."],
  quinque:["印欧 penkwe(五):希腊 pente、英语 five 同根。五=一只手——人类第一台计算器上的整数。","Indo-European penkwe: Greek pente, English five. Five = one hand — a whole number on humanity's first calculator."],
  sex:["印欧 sweks(六):希腊 hex、英语 six 同根。罗马学期 semester=sex menses,六个月——学期这个词里数着六。","Indo-European sweks: Greek hex, English six. A semester is sex menses, six months — the school term counts to six inside its own name."],
  septem:["印欧 septm(七):希腊 hepta、英语 seven 同根。罗马旧历三月起算,九月 September 本是第七月——历法改了,名字懒得改。","Indo-European septm: Greek hepta, English seven. Rome's old year began in March, so September was month seven — the calendar moved, the name never bothered."],
  octo:["印欧 okto(八):希腊 okto、英语 eight 同根。octopus 八条腿、octave 八度音——八从章鱼游到钢琴。","Indo-European okto: Greek okto, English eight. Eight legs on the octopus, eight notes in the octave — from sea to piano."],
  novem:["印欧 newn(九):英语 nine 同根。与 novus(新)碰巧同形——罗马人也分不清 November 是\"第九月\"还是\"新月\"。","Indo-European newn: English nine. It happens to look like novus (new) — even Romans could blur \"ninth month\" and \"new month.\""],
  decem:["印欧 dekm(十):希腊 deka、英语 ten 同根。十=两只手数完——于是十进制统治了人类算术。","Indo-European dekm: Greek deka, English ten. Ten = both hands used up — and so the decimal system conquered arithmetic."],
  centum:["印欧 kmtom(百):梵语 satam、英语 hundred 同根。语言学把印欧语分为 centum 与 satem 两大阵营——就以这个\"百\"字的读音划界。","Indo-European kmtom: Sanskrit satam, English hundred. Linguists split Indo-European into centum and satem camps — the border runs through this very word for 100."],
  mille:["词源不明的\"千\"。罗马兵走一千步(mille passus)记一里——英里 mile 是行军踩出来的单位。","A thousand of unknown origin. A thousand soldier-paces, mille passus, made one mile — a unit stamped out by marching feet."],
  albus:["印欧 albho-(白):阿尔卑斯山 Alps 或即\"白山\",英国古名 Albion 因白垩悬崖得名。相册 album 本是罗马的白色公告板。","Indo-European albho- (white): the Alps may be \"the white peaks,\" Albion is Britain's chalk-cliff name. An album was Rome's whitened notice-board."],
  niger:["词源不明的\"黑\"。它染黑了河流与国名(尼日尔、尼日利亚),也在 denigrate 里干着抹黑的活。","A black of unknown origin. It darkened rivers and nations (Niger, Nigeria) and still does the smearing inside denigrate."],
  ruber:["印欧 reudh-(红):英语 red、俄语 rudy 同根。人类最早命名的颜色就是血与火的红——语言学证实:红是各语言第一个\"彩色词\"。","Indo-European reudh-: English red, Russian rudy. The first color humans named was the red of blood and fire — linguists confirm: red is every language's first true color word."],
  viridis:["virere(草木青翠、有生机)的形容词:绿=活着的颜色。拉丁语的绿从来不只是色号,是生命力本身。","Adjective of virere (to be green and growing): green = the color of being alive. Latin green was never a swatch; it was vitality itself."],
  caeruleus:["从 caelum(天空)来:天色。奇怪的是罗马人也用它形容深海——地中海晴天里,海就是倒过来的天。","From caelum (sky): sky-colored. Oddly Romans used it for deep sea too — on a clear Mediterranean day, the sea is the sky turned over."],
  flavus:["印欧 bhel-(发亮):金黄是\"亮色\"。罗马诗人专用它写金发——维纳斯和阿波罗的头发都是 flavus。","Indo-European bhel- (to gleam): golden-yellow is \"the bright one.\" Poets kept it for hair — Venus and Apollo are both flavus."],
  purpureus:["希腊 porphyra:腓尼基骨螺染料。上万只海螺才染一件袍——泰尔紫贵过黄金,罗马仅皇帝可穿全紫,\"穿紫\"即\"称帝\"。","Greek porphyra: the Phoenician murex dye. Ten thousand shells for one robe — Tyrian purple outpriced gold; only the emperor wore it fully, and \"taking the purple\" meant taking the throne."],
  color:["celare(遮盖)家族:颜色是\"覆盖物的外表\"——古人眼里颜色不在物体里,而是罩在外面的一层皮。","Family of celare (to conceal): color is \"the covering's face\" — to the ancients color wasn't inside things but a skin laid over them."],
  /* ---- C1 L10 ---- */
  libertas:["liber(自由的)或出自\"人民、部族\"——自由=生为部族一员,不是奴隶。罗马给获释奴戴毡帽 pileus:美国\"自由帽\"的老祖宗。","liber (free) may come from \"the people, the tribe\" — free = born of the tribe, not enslaved. Freed slaves wore the pileus cap: ancestor of every liberty cap since."],
  felix:["与 fecundus(丰产)、femina 同根 dhe(i)-(哺乳):幸运原指\"果实累累\"。丰收即幸福——农民的词典里两者同义。","Kin of fecundus (fruitful), root dhe(i)-: lucky first meant fruit-laden. A good harvest was happiness — in a farmer's lexicon the two were one word."],
  laetus:["本是农事词:\"(田地)肥沃\"。土地肥→人心欢——罗马人的高兴是从地里长出来的。","A farming word first: \"(of fields) rich.\" Fat land → glad heart: Roman joy grew straight out of the soil."],
  victor:["vincere(战胜)+ -or(行为者):战胜者。凯旋式上 victor 面涂朱红扮朱庇特——胜利那天,人被允许暂当一次神。","vincere (to conquer) + -or: the conqueror. In triumph the victor painted his face red like Jupiter's statue — for one day, a man was allowed to play god."],
  patria:["pater 的土地:terra patria,父辈之地。贺拉斯写下 dulce et decorum est pro patria mori——为国捐躯何其甜美;一战诗人欧文引用它,加了三个字:古老的谎言。","pater's land: terra patria, the fathers' ground. Horace wrote dulce et decorum est pro patria mori — sweet it is to die for the fatherland; Wilfred Owen quoted it back with three words added: the old Lie."],
  memoria:["memor(记得的)之名词,印欧 (s)mer-(挂念)——英语 mourn(哀悼)同根:记住与哀悼,原是同一件心事。","Noun of memor (mindful), root (s)mer- (to care, dwell on) — English mourn is kin: remembering and mourning were one occupation of the heart."],
  fama:["fari(说)的产物:名声=人们嘴里说的你。维吉尔笔下 Fama 是长满眼睛耳朵翅膀的怪物,越飞越大——古罗马早就画出了\"谣言传播模型\"。","From fari (to speak): fame = what mouths say of you. Virgil's Fama is a winged monster covered in eyes and ears, growing as she flies — Rome drew the viral-rumor diagram first."],
  historia:["希腊 histor(亲见者、知情人),词根 weid-(看见)——英语 wit、wise 同根:历史的本义是\"亲眼所见之事的记录\"。","Greek histor (eyewitness, one who knows), root weid- (to see) — kin to wit and wise: history originally meant \"what the eyewitness saw, written down.\""],
  finis:["或从\"打进地里的界桩\"来。罗马有界神 Terminus,连朱庇特都让他三分——边界神圣不可移:finis 是钉进大地的词。","Perhaps \"the boundary stake driven in.\" Rome kept a god of boundaries, Terminus, whom even Jupiter respected — finis is a word hammered into the earth."],
  initium:["in(进入)+ ire(走):开始=\"走进去\"。罗马宗教把入教仪式叫 initia——人生每个开端都是一次入门。","in (into) + ire (to go): a beginning is a walking-in. Mystery cults called initiation initia — every beginning is a door crossed."],
  novus:["印欧 newo-(新):英语 new、希腊 neos 同根。罗马政坛骂白手起家者 homo novus\"新人\"——西塞罗把这蔑称戴成了勋章。","Indo-European newo-: English new, Greek neos. Politics sneered at the self-made homo novus, \"new man\" — Cicero wore the slur as a medal."],
  vetus:["印欧 wet-(年):本义\"有年头的\"。与希腊 etos(年)同根——老,就是攒了很多年。","Indo-European wet- (year): \"full of years.\" Kin to Greek etos (year) — to be old is simply to have collected many."],
  semper:["sem-(一)+ per(贯穿):\"一以贯之\"=永远。海军陆战队的 Semper Fidelis 让这个副词至今在军旗上飘着。","sem- (one) + per (throughout): \"in one unbroken run\" = always. Semper Fidelis keeps the adverb flying on flags today."],
  numquam:["ne(不)+ umquam(任何时候):把\"任何时候\"整个否定掉——拉丁语的\"从不\"是一次彻底的拒绝。","ne (not) + umquam (ever): negate \"ever\" entirely — Latin's never is a total refusal."],
  hodie:["hoc die(在这一天)口语连读成一个词:今天=\"就这天\"。日常口语把短语磨成单词——语言的包浆。","hoc die (\"on this day\") slurred into one word: today = \"this-day.\" Everyday speech wears phrases down into words — language's patina."],
  cras:["词源不明的\"明天\"。奥古斯丁忏悔自己老对上帝说 cras, cras——明天再改,明天再改:拖延症的忏悔录,一千六百年前已写好。","A tomorrow of unknown origin. Augustine confessed he kept telling God cras, cras — tomorrow, tomorrow: procrastination's memoir, written sixteen centuries ago."],
  heri:["印欧 dhghyes(昨天):希腊 chthes、英语 yester- 同根。昨天在各语言里都最容易磨损——过去的事,音也懒得发全。","Indo-European dhghyes: Greek chthes, English yester-. \"Yesterday\" erodes fastest in every language — for what's past, we barely finish the syllables."],
  tempus:["或从 temnein(切分)来:时间是\"被切开的段落\"——一段天时、一个时辰。拉丁警句 tempus fugit:时间在逃,你在追。","Perhaps from temnein (to cut): time is \"the cut-off stretch\" — a season, an hour. Tempus fugit: time is fleeing, and you are the pursuer."],
  annus:["印欧 at-no-(走过的一圈):年是\"绕完的一环\"——地球公转被古人直觉成一个圆。anniversary 就是\"年环的回转处\"。","Indo-European at-no- (the circuit walked): a year is \"the ring completed\" — the ancients felt the orbit as a circle. An anniversary is the ring's turning-point."],
  dies:["印欧 dyeu-(亮天)——和 deus(神)同根!白昼与神明是同一片光。dies 既是日子也是日光:活一天,就是借一天光。","Indo-European dyeu- (bright sky) — the same root as deus! Day and deity are one light. Dies is both the day and its daylight: to live a day is to borrow its light."],
  /* ---- C2 L1 ---- */
  musica:["希腊 mousike techne——\"缪斯的技艺\"。九位缪斯管歌、诗、史、天文:古人眼里凡需灵感之事皆是音乐的亲戚。","Greek mousike techne — \"the art of the Muses.\" Nine Muses ruled song, verse, history, astronomy: everything inspired was music's kin."],
  carmen:["canere(唱)的产物,古形 canmen。在罗马,法律条文、咒语、预言都叫 carmen——凡有韵律的语言皆是歌,歌皆有魔力。","From canere (to sing), older canmen. Laws, spells and prophecies were all carmina — rhythmic speech was song, and song was magic."],
  lyra:["希腊borrowed:传说婴儿赫尔墨斯捡龟壳绷弦制成第一把琴,送给阿波罗抵罪——乐器史第一桩以物易物。","A Greek loan: baby Hermes strung a tortoise shell and traded the first lyre to Apollo as an apology — music history's first barter."],
  chorda:["希腊 chorde(肠线)——最早的琴弦真是羊肠搓的。今天吉他手弹的 chord,词源里还带着牧场味。","Greek chorde (gut-string) — the first strings truly were twisted sheep-gut. Every guitar chord still smells faintly of the pasture."],
  sonus:["印欧 swen-(响):梵语 svana 同根。罗马修辞学讲究 sonus verborum——词语的声响:好文章要先好听。","Indo-European swen- (to sound): Sanskrit svana is kin. Rhetoric prized the sonus verborum — the ring of words: good prose must first sound good."],
  cantus:["canere(唱)的名词。高卢雄鸡报晓也叫 cantus——法语 chant、英语 chant 里仍是又唱又啼。","Noun of canere (to sing). The rooster's crow was cantus too — French and English chant still both sing and crow."],
  poeta:["希腊 poietes(制作者):诗人=\"做东西的人\"。希腊人不觉得诗是抒发,是手艺——诗是被\"做\"出来的。","Greek poietes (maker): the poet is \"one who makes.\" To Greeks a poem wasn't an outpouring but a craft — poems are made."],
  ars:["印欧 ar-(接合):技艺=把零件装配好的本事。与 arma(武器)同根——艺术与武器都讲究严丝合缝。","Indo-European ar- (to fit): skill is the knack of fitting parts together. Kin to arma — art and weaponry both demand a perfect fit."],
  tibia:["本义胫骨——最早的笛子就是掏空的兽骨或人骨。乐器名与骨头名至今同一个词:每支笛子都是一根会唱歌的骨。","Literally the shin-bone — the first flutes were hollowed animal (or human) bones. Instrument and bone still share the name: every flute is a bone that sings."],
  tympanum:["希腊 typtein(敲打)的产物:鼓=被敲的东西。酒神庆典的手鼓、耳朵里的鼓膜——凡是绷紧待敲的膜,都叫它。","From Greek typtein (to strike): the drum is the struck thing. Bacchic tambourines, the eardrum — any tight membrane awaiting a beat."],
  numerus:["印欧 nem-(分配):数是\"分出来的份额\"。希腊 nomos(法律)同根——数字与法律都在做同一件事:分清楚。","Indo-European nem- (to apportion): number is \"the share dealt out.\" Greek nomos (law) is kin — numbers and laws both exist to divide fairly."],
  versus:["vertere(转):农夫犁到田头调转犁头,一个来回就是一 versus——诗行是纸上的犁沟,写诗就是耕田。","From vertere (to turn): the ploughman turns at the field's end, and one furrow-length is a versus — a line of verse is a furrow on paper; to write is to plough."],
  fabula:["fari(说)+ 小词尾:\"被讲的小事\"。罗马人说 lupus in fabula——说曹操曹操到,字面是\"说狼狼到\"。","fari (to speak) with a diminutive: \"the little thing told.\" Romans said lupus in fabula — speak of the wolf and he appears."],
  scaena:["希腊 skene 本是\"帐篷\":早期演员在帐篷前表演,帐篷渐成布景房。舞台的祖先是一顶露营帐。","Greek skene meant tent: early actors played before one, and the tent grew into the stage-house. The theatre's ancestor is a camping tent."],
  theatrum:["希腊 theasthai(观看)+ 场所词尾:\"观看之地\"。希腊剧场依山而建,一万人同看一出戏——民主与戏剧共用同一个看台。","Greek theasthai (to behold) + place-ending: \"the seeing-place.\" Cut into hillsides, Greek theatres sat ten thousand — democracy and drama shared the same benches."],
  plausus:["plaudere(拍手)。罗马喜剧结尾演员喊 plaudite!(请鼓掌!)——两千年前的\"记得三连\"。","From plaudere (to clap). Roman comedies ended with the actor crying plaudite! — \"applaud!\": the like-and-subscribe of antiquity."],
  silva:["古意大利词:未开垦的林野。罗马人怕森林又靠森林——木材、猎物、神性都在林子里;silva 是文明的边界线。","An old Italic word: the untamed wood. Rome feared and needed the forest — timber, game and gods all lived there; silva marked civilization's edge."],
  arbor:["古意大利词,阴性——罗马人把树当女性看待:结果实的、庇荫的、哺育的。果树死了,农书作家会写\"她死了\"。","An old Italic word — and feminine: Rome saw trees as she — fruit-bearing, sheltering, nursing. When an orchard tree died, farm writers wrote \"she died.\""],
  fera:["印欧 ghwer-(野兽):希腊 ther 同根。ferus(野的)对 domus(家):罗马人把世界分成两半——屋里的,和野外的。","Indo-European ghwer- (wild beast): Greek ther is kin. ferus (wild) versus domus (home): Rome split the world in two — what lives under a roof, and what doesn't."],
  echo:["希腊神话:宁芙 Echo 因惹怒赫拉被罚只能重复别人的话尾,恋上纳西索斯而不得,形销骨立只剩声音——回声是一个女孩剩下的部分。","Greek myth: the nymph Echo, cursed by Hera to repeat only others' last words, loved Narcissus in vain and wasted to a voice — an echo is what remained of a girl."],
  /* ---- C2 L2 ---- */
  sponsa:["spondere(郑重许诺、奠酒立誓):新娘=\"被许诺之人\"。古礼订婚要奠酒起誓——婚姻先是一杯洒在祭坛上的酒。","From spondere (to pledge with libation): the bride is \"the promised one.\" Betrothal poured wine to the gods — marriage began as a libation."],
  sponsus:["同 sponsa 的阳性:新郎也是\"被许诺者\"——诺言双向,责任对等:罗马婚约里没有单方面的等待。","Masculine of sponsa: the groom too is \"the pledged one\" — the vow ran both ways; no one-sided waiting in a Roman betrothal."],
  nuptiae:["nubere(蒙上盖头)——罗马新娘罩火焰色的头纱 flammeum:婚礼这个词的字面义就是\"盖头纱\"。","From nubere (to veil oneself) — the Roman bride wore the flame-colored flammeum: the very word for wedding means \"taking the veil.\""],
  taeda:["松脂火把,婚礼专用——诗人直接用 taeda 代指婚姻。爱神若折断火把,便是姻缘尽了。","The pine torch of weddings — poets used taeda to mean marriage itself. When Love snapped the torch, the match was over."],
  flos:["印欧 bhle-(开花、涨起):英语 bloom、blossom 同根。flos 也指\"精华\"——面粉是谷之花,青春是命之花。","Indo-European bhle- (to bloom, swell): kin to bloom and blossom. flos also meant \"the finest part\" — flour is the grain's flower, youth is life's."],
  rosa:["经希腊 rhodon 自波斯远道而来——玫瑰与它的名字都产自东方。罗马宴会撒玫瑰如雨,\"sub rosa(玫瑰之下)\"至今意为保密。","Via Greek rhodon from Persia — flower and word both came from the East. Banquets rained petals; sub rosa, \"under the rose,\" still means in confidence."],
  lilium:["与希腊 leirion 同源,或出自古埃及/地中海底层语言——百合比字母表更早开在地中海岸。","Kin to Greek leirion, likely from an old Mediterranean tongue — lilies bloomed on these shores before alphabets did."],
  herba:["古意大利词:一切绿色草本。草药、牧草、毒草全是 herba——一个词里长满整个药圃。","An old Italic word: every green growing thing. Healing herbs, fodder and poisons were all herba — a whole physic garden in one word."],
  pratum:["词源不明的\"草场\"。西语 prado、法语 pré 由它铺开——马德里普拉多博物馆,字面就是\"草地博物馆\"。","A meadow of unknown origin. Spanish prado and French pré rolled out from it — the Prado Museum is literally \"the Meadow.\""],
  vipera:["vivus(活)+ parere(生产):古人以为毒蛇不下蛋、直接生小蛇——\"活产者\"。观察对了一半,名字全对。","vivus (alive) + parere (to bear): ancients thought vipers skipped eggs and bore live young — \"the live-bearer.\" Half-right observation, wholly right name."],
  venenum:["源自维纳斯 Venus:本义\"爱情药水\"!药水能救能杀,词义渐沉——从春药到毒药,一个词的黑化史。","From Venus herself: originally a love-potion! Potions cure or kill, and the word sank — from love-charm to poison, one word's fall from grace."],
  vulnus:["印欧 wel-(撕、击):伤口是\"被撕开处\"。维吉尔写爱情也用它——amoris vulnus,爱的伤口:身心通用。","Indo-European wel- (to tear, strike): a wound is \"the torn place.\" Virgil used it for love too — amoris vulnus: flesh and heart wound alike."],
  gaudium:["印欧 geh-(欢欣):希腊 ganymai 同根。宙斯的斟酒少年 Ganymedes 名字里就带着\"欢喜\"。","Indo-European geh- (to rejoice): Greek ganymai is kin. Ganymede, Zeus's cupbearer, carries \"joy\" inside his name."],
  lacrima:["希腊 dakryon(泪)的拉丁化,d 变 l——英语 tear 同根。奥德修斯闻歌而泣:英雄的眼泪,从荷马起就不丢人。","Greek dakryon Latinized, d turning to l — English tear is kin. Odysseus wept at a song: heroes' tears were never shameful, from Homer on."],
  dolor:["dolere(疼、哀)的名词。身体的痛与心里的悲共用一个词——拉丁语早就知道:心碎是真的会疼。","Noun of dolere (to hurt, grieve). Bodily pain and heart's sorrow share the word — Latin always knew heartbreak physically aches."],
  luctus:["lugere(哀悼)的名词。罗马服丧有严格礼制:着深衣、断宴乐、蓄须——luctus 是一段被制度化的悲伤。","Noun of lugere (to mourn). Roman mourning was regulated: dark dress, no feasts, beard untrimmed — luctus was grief given a rulebook."],
  funus:["词源不明。罗马葬礼是火把夜行的游行,祖先蜡像开道——funus 不是告别一个人,是全家族列队走进历史。","Origin unknown. A Roman funeral was a torchlit procession led by wax masks of the ancestors — not one farewell but a whole lineage marching into history."],
  sepulcrum:["sepelire(安葬)+ 场所词尾。罗马法规定墓地不可买卖侵占——locus religiosus,亡者一躺下,土地就归了神。","sepelire (to bury) + place-ending. Roman law made graves inviolable — locus religiosus: once the dead lay down, the ground belonged to the gods."],
  cinis:["与希腊 konis(尘)同根。罗马人火葬后把骨灰存入陶瓮——诗人写 et in cinere amor:连灰烬里也有爱。","Kin to Greek konis (dust). Ashes went into clay urns — poets wrote et in cinere amor: love survives even in the ash."],
  fatum:["fari(说)的分词:\"已被说出的\"。命运=神说过的话,说出即不可收回——罗马人的宿命论装在一个过去分词里。","Participle of fari (to speak): \"that which has been spoken.\" Fate is the gods' word once uttered, irrevocable — Roman fatalism packed into one past participle."],
  /* ---- C2 L3 ---- */
  inferi:["inferus(下方的)之复数:\"下面的那些\"——既指冥界又指冥界诸神。罗马人不爱直呼死后世界,用方位词代替:讳言即敬畏。","Plural of inferus (lower): \"those below\" — the underworld and its gods at once. Romans avoided naming the afterlife, pointing instead: euphemism as reverence."],
  regnum:["reg-(统治)+ 结果词尾:王权之地。共和罗马视 regnum 为脏词——指控某人\"图谋王权\"足以致死。","reg- (rule) + result-ending: the ruled realm. Republican Rome made regnum a dirty word — accusing a man of seeking it could get him killed."],
  imperium:["imperare(命令)的名词:合法的最高指挥权。罗马帝国 Imperium Romanum 字面即\"罗马的号令所及\"——帝国就是命令传到的边界。","Noun of imperare (to command): supreme lawful authority. Imperium Romanum means \"where Rome's command reaches\" — an empire is the range of an order."],
  thronus:["希腊 thronos:带脚凳的高座。荷马笔下神明各有 thronos——座位的高度就是地位的高度,词与椅子一起传给了欧洲王室。","Greek thronos: the high seat with footstool. Homer's gods each had one — rank was seat-height, and Europe's royalty inherited chair and word together."],
  sceptrum:["希腊 skeptron(拄杖):本是长老拄的手杖。荷马时代传杖发言——谁执杖谁说话:权杖的第一功能是话语权。","Greek skeptron: the elder's walking staff. In Homer the staff passed to whoever spoke — he who held it held the floor: the scepter's first power was the right to speak."],
  caverna:["cavus(空的)+ 场所词尾:空洞之地。维吉尔让埃涅阿斯经洞穴下冥府——罗马人相信大地的洞是两个世界的门。","cavus (hollow) + place-ending. Virgil sent Aeneas underworld through a cavern — caves were doorways between the worlds."],
  spelunca:["希腊 spelynx。奥古斯都时代著名的\"蓝洞\"就叫 spelunca——皇帝在洞窟里设宴:黑暗也能是奢侈品。","Greek spelynx. Emperor Tiberius dined in a famous grotto called Spelunca — darkness itself could be a luxury."],
  abyssus:["希腊 a-(无)+ byssos(底):无底之处。《圣经》拉丁本用它译\"深渊\"——创世之前的黑水就叫 abyssus。","Greek a- (without) + byssos (bottom). The Latin Bible used it for the deep — the dark waters before creation were abyssus."],
  vallis:["或与 volvere(卷)有关:山谷是\"大地卷出的褶\"。罗马谚语把安逸叫\"谷中生活\"——低处避风,也避见识。","Perhaps kin to volvere (to roll): a valley is earth's rolled fold. Low ground shelters from wind — and, said the proverb, from wisdom."],
  mons:["印欧 men-(突出、耸立):山=大地的突起。同根的 eminere(突出)、mentum(下巴)——凡是探出来的,都是这一族。","Indo-European men- (to jut): a mountain is earth thrust up. Kin: eminere (to stand out) and mentum (the chin) — whatever juts forward joins this family."],
  collis:["印欧 kel-(隆起):罗马建在七座 colles 上——七丘之城。丘不如山高,却正好够建一座永恒之城。","Indo-European kel- (to rise): Rome stood on seven colles — the City of Seven Hills. Lower than mountains, exactly high enough for an eternal city."],
  ripa:["或从\"陡断处\"来:河岸是大地对水的堤防。罗马法专设 ripa 条款——河岸属公,人人可泊船晒网:词里有古代的公共意识。","Perhaps \"the steep break\": the bank is land's levee against water. Roman law made riverbanks public — anyone might moor and dry nets: ancient commons in one word."],
  fluvius:["fluere(流)+ 名词尾:流动者。罗马视台伯河为神 pater Tiberinus——河是会奔跑的父亲。","fluere (to flow) + noun-ending: the flowing one. Rome worshipped the Tiber as pater Tiberinus — a river is a father who runs."],
  stagnum:["印欧 stag-(滴、停滞的水)。罗马工程师最恨它——排干沼泽是文明第一课;可皇家园林又人工挖 stagnum 造景:驯服的死水叫风景。","Indo-European stag- (dripping, standing water). Engineers hated it — draining swamps was civilization's first lesson; yet villas dug ornamental stagna: tamed still water became scenery."],
  palus:["印欧 pel-(灌注):积水之地。罗马城起点就是排干一片 palus 建广场——罗马广场的地基下,躺着一个被战胜的沼泽。","Indo-European pel- (to pour): the flooded ground. Rome began by draining a palus to build the Forum — beneath the Forum lies a conquered marsh."],
  nebula:["印欧 nebh-(云雾):德语 Nebel、希腊 nephos 同根。天文学借它命名星云——人类把地上的雾挂到了星空。","Indo-European nebh- (mist): German Nebel, Greek nephos. Astronomy borrowed it for star-clouds — earth's fog hung among the stars."],
  frigus:["希腊 rhigos(寒颤)同根。罗马医学把病分冷热,frigus 是死亡的方向——尸体先失去的就是温度。","Kin to Greek rhigos (shivering). Roman medicine sorted ills into hot and cold; frigus pointed toward death — warmth is what a body loses first."],
  gelu:["印欧 gel-(冻):英语 cold、cool 同根异路。阿尔卑斯以北的 gelu 让罗马士兵发明了裤子——寒冷改变了时尚。","Indo-European gel- (to freeze): cold and cool are its Germanic cousins. Alpine gelu drove Roman soldiers into trousers — frost rewrote fashion."],
  horror:["horrere:毛发直立、皮肤起栗——horror 首先是生理反应,先起鸡皮疙瘩,后才有恐怖片。","horrere: hair standing, skin prickling — horror was first a bodily event: goosebumps came before the film genre."],
  custos:["词源不明的\"看守者\"。尤维纳利斯之问流传两千年:quis custodiet ipsos custodes?——谁来看守看守者?","A guardian of unknown origin. Juvenal's question still stands: quis custodiet ipsos custodes? — who guards the guards?"],
  /* ---- C2 L4 ---- */
  portitor:["portus(渡口)+ -itor:渡口收费员——卡戎就是冥河海关:一枚小钱 obolus 放行,死人也要交过路费。","portus (crossing) + -itor: the toll-keeper of the ford — Charon is death's customs officer: one obol to pass; even the dead pay tolls."],
  cymba:["希腊 kymbe(碗、小舟):碗与船同名——第一只船就是一只放大的碗。诗人专用它称卡戎的渡船。","Greek kymbe (bowl, skiff): bowl and boat shared a name — the first boat was a bowl enlarged. Poets kept it for Charon's ferry."],
  ratis:["本义\"木排\":绑在一起的原木。奥德修斯离开卡吕普索时造的就是 ratis——英雄的最低配置:几根木头加一身勇气。","Literally lashed logs: a raft. Odysseus left Calypso on a ratis — a hero's minimum kit: some timber and courage."],
  merces:["merx(商品)家族:报酬是\"劳务的售价\"。西语敬称 usted=vuestra merced(您的恩典)——今天西语每喊一声\"您\",都在付一次 merces。","Family of merx (goods): pay is labor's price. Spanish usted = vuestra merced, \"your grace\" — every Spanish \"usted\" still pays out this word."],
  nummus:["希腊 nomisma:nomos(共同约定)之物——钱的本质被古希腊一语道破:硬币值钱,只因大家说好它值钱。","Greek nomisma: the thing of nomos, common consent — Greece named money's secret: a coin has value because everyone agrees it does."],
  latratus:["latrare(吠)之名词,拟声词根——狗自己配的音。刻耳柏洛斯的 latratus 三倍立体声,俄耳甫斯用琴声把它唱成了鼾声。","Noun of latrare (to bark), echoic — the dog dubbed itself. Cerberus barked in triple stereo till Orpheus's lyre turned it to snoring."],
  guttur:["拟声:咕噜咕噜的喉音就是 gut-tur。词根模仿吞咽声——喉咙的名字是喉咙自己发出的。","Echoic: the gurgle in your throat says gut-tur. The word imitates swallowing — the throat pronounced its own name."],
  ungula:["unguis(指甲)+ 小词尾:\"小指甲\"→爪与蹄。同根希腊 onyx——玛瑙 onyx 因纹似指甲得名:宝石里嵌着一片指甲。","unguis (nail) + diminutive: \"little nail\" → claw and hoof. Greek kin onyx — the gem is named for its fingernail bands: a nail set in stone."],
  cauda:["词源不明的\"尾巴\"。音乐术语 coda(尾声)、法语 queue(队列)都从它甩出——排队是人类的尾巴。","A tail of unknown origin. Musical coda and French queue both swing from it — a waiting line is humanity's tail."],
  pellis:["印欧 pel-(皮、膜):英语 fell(兽皮)、film(薄膜!)同根——电影 film 的祖先是一张皮。","Indo-European pel- (skin, membrane): kin to fell and — film! The movies' film was first a skin."],
  belua:["词源不明,专指\"庞大得反常的兽\"——鲸、象、海怪。罗马人第一次见大象,叫它 belua 卢卡尼亚的——\"卢卡尼亚巨兽\"。","Origin unknown; the abnormally huge beast — whale, elephant, sea-monster. Meeting elephants in war, Romans called them \"Lucanian beluae.\""],
  draco:["希腊 drakon,来自 derkesthai(死死盯住):龙=\"凝视者\"——传说中巨蛇目光不眨。看守金羊毛的正是一条不合眼的 draco。","Greek drakon, from derkesthai (to stare): the dragon is \"the starer\" — serpents never blink. The Fleece's guardian was a draco that never closed its eyes."],
  gigas:["希腊神话:Gigantes 是大地女神盖亚之子,与奥林匹斯神大战落败——\"巨人\"从诞生起就是败给诸神的一方。","Greek myth: the Gigantes were Earth's sons who warred on Olympus and lost — \"giant\" has meant heaven's defeated rival from its birth."],
  nympha:["希腊 nymphe:同时指\"新娘\"与\"泉林仙女\"——待嫁少女与自然精灵共用一个词:古希腊把青春本身看作半个神。","Greek nymphe: both \"bride\" and \"nature-spirit\" — marriageable girl and woodland goddess in one word: Greece saw youth itself as half-divine."],
  anima:["与 animus 同根 ane-(呼吸)的阴性:那口维持生命的气。人咽气叫 animam efflare——把 anima 呼出去:死亡是最后一次呼气。","Feminine of the breath-root ane-: the life-keeping breath. Dying was animam efflare — breathing the anima out: death is the last exhale."],
  spectrum:["specere(看)+ -trum:\"显现之物\"→幻象、幽灵。牛顿 1671 年借它命名棱镜彩带——科学从鬼故事借了个词。","specere (to look) + -trum: \"the appearing thing\" → apparition. In 1671 Newton borrowed it for the prism's band — science took a word from ghost stories."],
  phantasma:["希腊 phainein(使显现)家族:幻影=\"显给你看的东西\"。《圣经》里门徒见耶稣海上行走,喊的就是 phantasma!","Family of Greek phainein (to make appear): the phantom is \"what is shown to you.\" Seeing Jesus walk the sea, the disciples cried phantasma!"],
  turba:["希腊 tyrbe(喧闹)同源:人一多就乱,乱就是 turba——人群、骚乱、漩涡共用一词:混乱不分固体液体。","Kin to Greek tyrbe (uproar): crowds breed disorder, and disorder is turba — mob, riot and whirl share the word: chaos ignores states of matter."],
  multitudo:["multus(多)+ -tudo:抽象的\"多\"。罗马政治文献里 multitudo 常带贬义——\"乌合之众\":数量一大,名字就危险。","multus (many) + -tudo: manyness itself. In political prose multitudo often sneered — \"the mob\": numbers alone made the word dangerous."],
  ordo:["本义织机上的\"经线排列\"、剧场的\"座位排\"。罗马把元老阶层叫 ordo senatorius——社会秩序的本义,是人人有个座位。","First the warp-rows on a loom, the seat-rows in a theatre. Senators were the ordo senatorius — social order originally meant everyone having a row to sit in."],
  /* ---- C2 L5 ---- */
  oblivio:["ob(抹过)+ levis(平滑):遗忘是\"把刻痕抹平\"——记忆是蜡板,忘记就是用手把字迹抚平。","ob (over) + levis (smooth): to forget is to smooth the wax — memory was a wax tablet, and forgetting the palm that erases it."],
  flumen:["fluere(流)+ -men:\"流动\"本身作名词。fluvius 是具体某条河,flumen 更抽象——\"那股流\"。拉丁语连河都分\"这条河\"与\"流本身\"。","fluere (to flow) + -men: flowing itself as a noun. fluvius is a particular river; flumen is \"the current\" — Latin even split the river from its flow."],
  rivus:["印欧 rei-(流、划):小溪。罗马邻里为渠水打官司,同用一条 rivus 的人叫 rivales——竞争者 rival 的第一案发现场是灌溉渠。","Indo-European rei- (to flow, cut): the brook. Neighbors sued over channel water; sharers of one rivus were rivales — the first rivalry was over irrigation."],
  lacus:["印欧 laku-(积水):苏格兰 loch、爱尔兰 lough 同根——湖的名字绕过罗马直通凯尔特山谷。","Indo-European laku- (pooled water): Scottish loch and Irish lough are kin — the lake's name flows past Rome into Celtic glens."],
  gurges:["印欧 gwere-(吞):漩涡是\"水的喉咙\"。同根 vorare(吞吃)——大水吞人,词根早有预告。","Indo-European gwere- (to swallow): the whirlpool is water's throat. Kin vorare (to devour) — the root always warned that water eats."],
  vortex:["vertere(转)的产物,与 vertigo(眩晕)同胎:水打转是 vortex,头打转是 vertigo——里外都在转。","From vertere (to turn), twin of vertigo: water spinning is a vortex, a head spinning is vertigo — turning without and within."],
  gutta:["词源不明的\"一滴\"。罗马格言 gutta cavat lapidem——水滴石穿:最小的名词,配了最持久的动词。","A drop of unknown origin. The proverb: gutta cavat lapidem — the drop hollows the stone: the smallest noun with the most persistent verb."],
  umor:["umere(潮湿)之名词。古医学\"四体液说\"用它两千年——血、痰、黄胆、黑胆配平了才健康;心情不好叫\"体液失调\":幽默 humor 是医学术语退休再就业。","Noun of umere (to be moist). The four humors ruled medicine for two millennia — health was fluids in balance; a bad mood was \"humoral imbalance\": humor is a retired medical term with a second career."],
  vapor:["或与希腊 kapnos(烟)远亲。罗马浴场的蒸汽室叫 vaporarium——帝国的放松方式:躺进一个词根里出汗。","Perhaps distant kin of Greek kapnos (smoke). The baths' steam-room was the vaporarium — imperial relaxation: sweating inside an etymology."],
  ardor:["ardere(燃烧)之名词:灼热→热情。罗马人形容恋爱、雄心、演说全用\"燃烧\"——心理学之前,内心世界全靠火来比喻。","Noun of ardere (to burn): blaze → passion. Love, ambition, oratory — all \"burned\" in Latin: before psychology, the inner world ran on fire metaphors."],
  aestus:["印欧 aidh-(烧):既指热浪也指潮涌——地中海正午,热与潮一起扑岸:一个词根同时管温度与海况。","Indo-European aidh- (to burn): both heat-wave and tide-surge — at Mediterranean noon heat and swell strike together: one root running temperature and sea-state."],
  glacies:["印欧 gel-(冻)之名词。汉尼拔翻越阿尔卑斯损兵过半,敌人不是罗马是 glacies——冰第一次改写了战争史。","Noun of gel- (to freeze). Hannibal lost half his army crossing the Alps to no Roman blade but to glacies — ice's first rewrite of military history."],
  sulphur:["地中海底层古词,先于希腊拉丁。维苏威火山喷发时,普林尼记下漫天硫气——sulphur 是庞贝末日的气味。","An old pre-Greek Mediterranean word. As Vesuvius erupted, Pliny recorded the sulfur reek — sulphur is the smell of Pompeii's last day."],
  sitis:["印欧 dhgwhei-(消耗、干竭):渴是\"身体的干旱\"。坦塔罗斯站在水中永远够不到水——希腊人给\"渴\"造了个专属地狱。","Indo-European dhgwhei- (to waste, parch): thirst is the body's drought. Tantalus stood in water he could never drink — Greece built a private hell just for thirst."],
  fames:["词源不明的\"饥饿\"。罗马诗人称黄金欲为 auri sacra fames——\"对黄金的可诅咒的饥饿\":胃口一词,喂什么都行。","A hunger of unknown origin. Poets damned gold-lust as auri sacra fames — \"accursed hunger for gold\": an appetite word that feeds on anything."],
  poculum:["po-(喝,印欧 po(i)-)+ 器具词尾:饮器。梵语 pibati(他喝)同根——从恒河到台伯河,人类举的是同一只杯。","Drink-root po(i)- + tool-ending: the drinking vessel. Sanskrit pibati (he drinks) is kin — from Ganges to Tiber, mankind raised one cup."],
  potio:["potare(饮)之名词:一切喝下去的东西——药、酒、毒不分。它分裂成英语 potion 与 poison:一杯下肚,是魔法还是谋杀,全看剂量。","Noun of potare (to drink): anything swallowed — medicine, wine or venom. It split into potion and poison: magic or murder, a matter of dose."],
  limus:["印欧 (s)lei-(黏滑):英语 slime、slippery 同根。尼罗河的沃泥也是 limus——同一个词,又脏又肥:文明就种在烂泥里。","Indo-European (s)lei- (slimy): kin to slime and slippery. The Nile's rich mud was limus too — one word both filthy and fertile: civilization was planted in muck."],
  murmur:["完全重叠的拟声词:mur-mur——溪水、人语、远雷、蜂群共用此声。印欧人已这么叠,梵语 marmara 同款:五千年前的拟声至今在用。","Perfect reduplicated onomatopoeia: mur-mur — brook, whisper, far thunder, swarming bees. Indo-Europeans doubled it already; Sanskrit marmara matches: a five-thousand-year-old sound effect still in service."],
  profundum:["pro(向前)+ fundus(底):\"直抵底部\"。罗马人用它形容海、夜、学问——凡是一眼望不到底的,都是 profundum。","pro (forth) + fundus (bottom): reaching the very bottom. Used of sea, night and learning — whatever the eye cannot fathom is profundum."],
  /* ---- C2 L6 ---- */
  iudex:["ius(法)+ dicere(宣告):法官=\"宣告法之人\"。罗马法官开庭三个字 do, dico, addico——我准许、我宣告、我裁定:权力浓缩成三个动词。","ius (law) + dicere (to declare): the judge speaks the law. His formula was three verbs — do, dico, addico: I grant, I declare, I adjudge."],
  iudicium:["iudex 的行为名词:审判。罗马审判在露天广场,公民围观——iudicium 既是程序也是剧场:正义必须被看见。","Act-noun of iudex: the trial. Held in the open Forum before crowds — iudicium was procedure and theatre at once: justice had to be seen."],
  lex:["或从 legere(选、读)来:法律是\"被选定并公开宣读的条文\"。罗马第一部成文法《十二表法》刻铜立于广场——法律从诞生起就必须让人读到。","Perhaps from legere (to choose, read): law is \"what was chosen and read aloud.\" The Twelve Tables stood in bronze in the Forum — from birth, law had to be readable."],
  ius:["印欧 yewes-(神圣的约束):法最早是宗教誓约。ius 又指\"肉汤\"(另一词源)——罗马学童也拿这对同形词开玩笑。","Indo-European yewes- (sacred binding): law began as religious oath. A homonym ius meant \"broth\" — Roman schoolboys joked about it too."],
  iustitia:["iustus(合法的)+ -itia:正义作为品质。正义女神蒙眼持剑持天平的形象是罗马遗产——蒙眼:法律面前不看脸。","iustus (lawful) + -itia: justice as a quality. The blindfolded Lady with sword and scales is Rome's bequest — blindfolded: the law sees no faces."],
  crimen:["cernere(筛选、判别)家族:crimen 先是\"指控\",后才是\"罪行\"——先有人喊冤,才有罪名:词序即程序正义。","Family of cernere (to sift, decide): crimen was first the charge, only later the crime — accusation precedes guilt: due process built into word order."],
  culpa:["词源不明。罗马法区分 dolus(故意)与 culpa(过失)——两千年后各国刑法还在用这条线。忏悔词 mea culpa 让它进了日常语。","Origin unknown. Roman law split dolus (intent) from culpa (negligence) — criminal codes still draw that line. The confession mea culpa carried it into daily speech."],
  poena:["希腊 poine:血债的赎金——最早的\"刑罚\"是凶手付给死者家属的和解金。从赔钱到受苦,poena 记录了正义的进化。","Greek poine: blood-money paid to a victim's kin — punishment began as a settlement. From paying to suffering, poena logs the evolution of justice."],
  carcer:["或借自腓尼基:围栏。罗马监狱不关人服刑——只押候审与待决:carcer 是过道,不是终点;\"坐牢\"是后世的发明。","Perhaps Phoenician: an enclosure. Roman jails held men only for trial or execution — carcer was a corridor, not a sentence; prison time is a later invention."],
  catena:["词源不明的\"链\"。罗马凯旋式上战俘戴金链游行——耻辱也分等级:锁链的材质是身份的最后声明。","A chain of unknown origin. Captive kings marched in triumph wearing golden chains — even humiliation had ranks: the metal of your shackles was your last statement."],
  vinculum:["vincire(捆绑)+ 工具词尾。数学的分数线、化学的键、法律的效力全叫 vinculum——文明的进步史就是一部\"看不见的绳子\"目录。","vincire (to bind) + tool-ending. The fraction bar, the chemical bond, legal force — all vinculum: civilization is a catalogue of invisible ropes."],
  veritas:["verus(真)+ -itas,印欧 weros——英语 very 同根:说 very true 等于把\"真\"说了两遍。罗马谚语 in vino veritas:酒后吐真言。","verus (true) + -itas, root weros — English very is kin: \"very true\" says true twice. In vino veritas: wine lets the truth out."],
  testis:["或从 tri-stis(第三方站立者)来:证人=\"站在两造之外的第三人\"。作证 testari 要摸着圣物起誓——真话是向神担保的。","Perhaps from tri-stis, \"the third party standing by\": a witness stands outside both sides. To testify one swore on sacred objects — truth was underwritten by the gods."],
  causa:["词源不明。罗马律师称案件为 causa,辩护叫 causam dicere(为案由发言)——原因与案件同词:找原因,就是打官司。","Origin unknown. Lawyers called a case causa; to plead was causam dicere — cause and case were one word: to seek causes is to litigate."],
  sententia:["sentire(感受)+ -entia:判决的本义竟是\"法官的感受\"——罗马人诚实得吓人:再严密的法条,最后一步仍是人心。","sentire (to feel) + -entia: a sentence is literally \"what the judge feels\" — Rome was frighteningly honest: after all the statutes, the last step is a human heart."],
  innocens:["in(不)+ nocens(加害的):无辜=\"未曾伤人\"。拉丁语的清白是行为记录,不是内心状态——没伤过人,就是好人。","in (not) + nocens (harming): innocent = \"having harmed no one.\" Latin innocence is a track record, not a state of soul — do no harm, and you are good."],
  nocens:["nocere(伤害)的分词:正在害人的。与 nex(横死)同根 nek-——伤害与死亡在词根深处握手。","Participle of nocere (to harm). Root nek- also gives nex (violent death) — harm and death shake hands deep in the etymology."],
  supplicium:["sub(向下)+ plicare(折弯):跪下。同一姿势两种命运——向神跪是祈祷 supplicatio,被按跪是处决 supplicium:膝盖一弯,词义两开。","sub (down) + plicare (to bend): kneeling. One posture, two fates — kneeling to pray is supplicatio, being forced down is execution: the knee bends, the word forks."],
  labor:["本义\"重压下的踉跄\":labor 先指苦难,后指劳动。维吉尔写 labor omnia vincit——劳动征服一切:罗马把苦难升格成了美德。","First \"staggering under a load\": labor meant hardship before it meant work. Virgil wrote labor omnia vincit — toil conquers all: Rome promoted suffering into virtue."],
  rota:["印欧 ret-(滚):梵语 ratha(战车)同根。中世纪的\"命运之轮\"Rota Fortunae 转起国王与乞丐——轮子一转,座次重排。","Indo-European ret- (to roll): Sanskrit ratha (chariot) is kin. The medieval Rota Fortunae spun kings down and beggars up — one turn, new seating."],
  /* ---- C2 L7 ---- */
  dominus:["domus(家)+ 主人词尾:一家之主。帝国晚期皇帝要求被称 dominus——从\"家主\"到\"主上\",一个词量出自由的流失。","domus (house) + master-ending: head of the household. Late emperors demanded the title dominus — from householder to Lord, one word measures freedom draining away."],
  domina:["dominus 的阴性:女主人。罗马主妇掌家钥、管账目——domina 不是装饰称号,是一份实权职位。","Feminine of dominus: mistress of the house. She held the keys and the ledgers — domina was no honorific but an office with real power."],
  maiestas:["maior(更大)+ -estas:\"更大者的地位\"。冒犯它是大罪 crimen maiestatis——\"大不敬\"罪名从罗马一路用到近代宫廷。","maior (greater) + -estas: the standing of the greater. To wound it was crimen maiestatis — lèse-majesté, a charge that ran from Rome to the modern courts of kings."],
  potentia:["posse(能够)家族:潜在的能力。与 potestas(合法权力)相对——罗马政治哲学分得清:能做什么,和可以做什么,是两个词。","Family of posse (to be able): capacity. Set against potestas, lawful power — Roman thought kept them apart: what you CAN do and what you MAY do are different words."],
  potestas:["posse(能)+ -tas:官职赋予的合法权力。罗马官员权力有任期有边界——potestas 天生带着\"到期\"属性:权力是借的,不是有的。","posse (can) + -tas: power conferred by office, bounded and term-limited — potestas expires by design: power is borrowed, never owned."],
  honor:["词源不明。罗马的 honor 是官职本身——cursus honorum\"荣誉之路\"就是升官图:荣誉不是感觉,是岗位。","Origin unknown. In Rome honor WAS public office — the cursus honorum, \"path of honors,\" was the career ladder: honor wasn't a feeling but a post."],
  superbia:["super(在上)+ -bia:\"把自己放在上面\"。罗马末代暴君绰号 Superbus(傲慢王)——因他,\"骄傲\"在拉丁语里几乎全是贬义。","super (above) + -bia: setting oneself on top. Rome's last tyrant was nicknamed Superbus — thanks to him, pride in Latin stayed mostly a vice."],
  ira:["或与希腊 hieros(激烈的)远亲。塞内加写《论愤怒》称 ira 为\"短暂的疯狂\"——哲学家给情绪下的最狠定义之一。","Perhaps distant kin of Greek hieros (violent). Seneca's On Anger called ira \"a brief madness\" — philosophy's harshest verdict on an emotion."],
  invidia:["in(向、盯)+ videre(看):嫉妒=盯着看。罗马人怕\"恶眼\"evil eye——被嫉妒的目光盯上会遭灾:invidia 是一种视线攻击。","in (upon) + videre (to look): envy is staring. Romans feared the evil eye — a jealous gaze could curse you: invidia was an attack by looking."],
  misericordia:["miser(不幸的)+ cor(心):\"为不幸者而动的心\"。斯多噶派警惕它,基督教尊它为最高德——一个词,两大哲学掰了几百年。","miser (wretched) + cor (heart): the heart moved by misery. Stoics distrusted it; Christianity crowned it — one word, two philosophies wrestling for centuries."],
  clementia:["clemens(温和的),或与\"倾斜\"有关:仁慈是\"愿意俯身\"。恺撒以 clementia 待政敌闻名——宽恕作为统治术,他玩到了极致。","clemens (mild), perhaps from \"leaning\": mercy is a willingness to bend down. Caesar made clementia his brand, pardoning enemies — forgiveness as statecraft, played to perfection."],
  preces:["印欧 prek-(开口求):梵语 prasna(问题)同根——祈祷与提问同根:向神开口,人类的第一个问题就是恳求。","Indo-European prek- (to ask): Sanskrit prasna (question) is kin — prayer and question share a root: mankind's first question was a plea."],
  votum:["vovere(向神许愿)的分词:誓愿=\"已向神许下的\"。罗马人许愿要还愿,神庙挂满还愿牌 ex voto——与神做生意,契约精神拉满。","Participle of vovere (to vow): the thing promised to a god. Vows had to be repaid — temples hung with ex voto plaques: doing business with heaven, contracts honored in full."],
  templum:["本义:占卜官用杖在天空划出的观测区——先有天上的 templum,后有地上的神庙:圣域最初是一块被框起来的天空。","Originally the sky-sector an augur marked out with his staff — the heavenly templum came before any building: sacred space began as a framed piece of sky."],
  ara:["古拉丁 asa:垫高的祭火台。罗马和约在 ara 前宣誓,逃亡者抱 ara 求庇护——祭坛是神界的使馆:碰到它,人间law暂停。","Old Latin asa: the raised fire-table. Treaties were sworn at the ara; fugitives clasped it for asylum — an altar was heaven's embassy: touch it, and earthly law paused."],
  sacrificium:["sacer(神圣)+ facere(做):\"把东西做成神的\"。祭品一经奉献即属神界,人不可再碰——sacrificium 是所有权向天的转移。","sacer (holy) + facere (to make): making a thing the god's. Once offered it left human ownership forever — sacrifice is a title transfer to heaven."],
  thesaurus:["希腊 thesauros(库房):存放城邦献金的屋子。文艺复兴学者借它命名词汇集——\"词库\":每个同义词典都自称一座金库。","Greek thesauros: the treasury-house of a city's offerings. Renaissance scholars borrowed it for word-hoards — every thesaurus claims to be a vault."],
  opes:["印欧 op-(劳作、收获):财富=干出来的收成。单数 ops 是丰收女神 Ops——罗马人拜的\"财神\"其实是庄稼。","Indo-European op- (to work, produce): wealth is harvest worked for. Singular Ops was the goddess of plenty — Rome's \"god of riches\" was really the crop."],
  diadema:["希腊 diadein(缠绕):亚历山大征服波斯后戴上白色发带 diadema——欧洲的王冠传统,起点是一条布条。","Greek diadein (to bind round): after conquering Persia, Alexander tied on the white headband diadema — Europe's crown tradition began as a strip of cloth."],
  numen:["nuere(点头)+ -men:神的点头。罗马人不需要神显形——一阵风、一个征兆就是 numen:神性最小单位,一次颔首。","nuere (to nod) + -men: the god's nod. Romans needed no epiphany — a gust, an omen was numen: divinity's smallest unit, one inclination of the head."],
  /* ---- C2 L8 ---- */
  eloquentia:["e(出)+ loqui(说):\"把话说出去\"的功夫。西塞罗靠它无兵无财而权倾罗马——共和国最锋利的武器是舌头。","e (out) + loqui (to speak): the craft of speech that carries. Cicero rose armyless and fortuneless on it — the Republic's sharpest weapon was the tongue."],
  suavitas:["印欧 swad-(甜):英语 sweet、梵语 svadu 同根。演说的\"甜\"能左右判决——persuade(说服)字面就是\"用甜浸透\"。","Indo-European swad- (sweet): kin to sweet and Sanskrit svadu. Sweetness of speech swayed verdicts — persuade literally means \"to sweeten through.\""],
  dulcis:["或与希腊 glykys(甜,→glucose)远亲。罗马人称爱人 dulcissime——\"最甜的人\":甜是拉丁情话的最高级。","Perhaps distant kin of Greek glykys (sweet → glucose). Lovers were addressed dulcissime, \"sweetest one\" — sweetness was Latin endearment's superlative."],
  amarus:["或与梵语 amla(酸)远亲。意大利的苦味酒就叫 amaro——欧洲人保留了\"苦\"的品味:一杯 amaro,词源当开胃酒。","Perhaps kin to Sanskrit amla (sour). Italy's bitter liqueur is the amaro — Europe kept its taste for bitterness: an etymology served as aperitivo."],
  mollis:["印欧 mel-(磨软):英语 mill(磨坊)、meal 同根。诗歌里 mollis 形容柔诗、软语、春风——磨出来的软,才是文明的软。","Indo-European mel- (to grind soft): kin to mill and meal. Poets used mollis for tender verse, soft words, spring wind — softness ground fine is civilization's kind."],
  durus:["或从\"树木\"词根 deru- 来(英语 tree、true 同根!):硬如硬木,truth 与 durus 都是\"像橡树一样结实\"。","Perhaps from deru- (tree — kin to tree and true!): hard as heartwood; truth and durus both mean \"oak-solid.\""],
  blandus:["词源不明:温言软语的。blandiri(哄)——罗马人清楚甜言的双刃:blandus 既是\"温柔\"也是\"谄媚\":一词自带警告。","Origin unknown: soft-spoken. From blandiri (to coax) — Rome knew sweet talk cuts both ways: blandus means both gentle and flattering: a word with its own warning label."],
  ferox:["ferus(野)+ -ox(目光/性情词尾):\"野兽眼神的\"。既可骂残暴也可赞骁勇——罗马人对\"猛\"的矛盾态度全在这个词里。","ferus (wild) + -ox (of eye/temper): \"with a wild beast's look.\" An insult or a compliment — Rome's ambivalence about fierceness lives in one word."],
  mirus:["印欧 smei-(微笑):惊奇的词根竟是\"笑\"——看见奇妙之物,人先笑后叹。miracle 与 smile 是失散的兄弟。","Indo-European smei- (to smile): wonder's root is laughter — faced with marvels we smile before we gasp. Miracle and smile are long-lost brothers."],
  mirabilis:["mirus + -abilis:\"值得惊叹的\"。中世纪游记满篇 mirabilia(奇观录)——马可·波罗之前,欧洲人靠这个词想象世界。","mirus + -abilis: worth wondering at. Medieval travel books listed mirabilia — before Marco Polo, Europe imagined the world through this word."],
  stupor:["stupere(被打懵、僵住):过度的惊吓让人变石头——美杜莎的受害者得的就是终极 stupor。","stupere (to be stunned rigid): shock enough turns you to stone — Medusa's victims suffered the ultimate stupor."],
  admiratio:["ad(朝向)+ mirari(惊叹):对准某物的惊叹。亚里士多德说哲学始于惊奇——拉丁人把这份惊奇装进了 admiratio。","ad (toward) + mirari (to wonder): wonder aimed at something. Aristotle said philosophy begins in wonder — Latin bottled that wonder as admiratio."],
  desiderium:["de + sidus(星):一说源于占星——\"从星象中寻求(而不得)\":渴望是对着星空要一个回不来的东西。拉丁语最浪漫的词源之一。","de + sidus (star): perhaps from augury — \"to seek from the stars (in vain)\": longing is asking the sky for what won't return. One of Latin's most romantic etymologies."],
  spes:["印欧 spe-(伸展、指望):希望是\"向前伸的心\"。潘多拉盒底剩下的就是 Spes——罗马给她建庙:绝境里的最后一位女神。","Indo-European spe- (to stretch toward): hope is the heart reaching forward. What stayed in Pandora's jar was Spes — Rome built her temples: the last goddess left in the dark."],
  promissum:["pro(向前)+ mittere(送出):承诺=\"预先送出的话\"。话已出门,人必须跟上——拉丁语的守信,是给自己的话跑腿。","pro (ahead) + mittere (to send): a promise is speech sent ahead. The word has left the house; you must follow it — keeping faith is running errands for your own words."],
  condicio:["con + dicere(共同说定):条件=\"双方一起说好的\"。冥王给俄耳甫斯的 condicio 只有一句——古典悲剧的规则:条款越简单,违约越致命。","con + dicere (to declare together): terms both sides spoke. Hades gave Orpheus a single condicio — tragedy's rule: the simpler the clause, the deadlier the breach."],
  pactum:["pacisci(缔和)的分词,与 pax(和平)同根:契约=\"已缔结的和平\"。拉丁法谚 pacta sunt servanda(契约必守)至今是国际法根基。","Participle of pacisci (to make peace), kin to pax: a pact is peace concluded. Pacta sunt servanda — agreements must be kept — still anchors international law."],
  foedus:["与 fides(信任)同根 bheidh-:盟约是\"制度化的信任\"。罗马与诸邦的 foedera 织成意大利联盟——条约网就是帝国的第一张骨架。","Root bheidh-, kin to fides: a treaty is trust institutionalized. Rome's foedera wove Italy into alliance — the treaty-web was the empire's first skeleton."],
  gratia:["gratus(令人愉悦的)+ -ia:先是\"魅力\",后是\"恩惠\",再是\"感谢\"——从希腊美惠三女神到西语 gracias:优雅、恩典与道谢,竟是一个词的三次转身。","gratus (pleasing) + -ia: first charm, then favor, then thanks — from the three Graces to Spanish gracias: elegance, grace and gratitude are one word turning three times."],
  venia:["或与 Venus、venerari(敬拜)同根 wen-(想要、爱):宽恕是\"爱的让步\"——求 venia 就是求爱意网开一面。","Perhaps root wen- (to desire, love) with Venus and venerari: pardon is love's concession — to beg venia is to ask affection to look away."],
  /* ---- C2 L9 ---- */
  aspectus:["ad + specere(看):投向某物的目光。占星术语 aspectus(星位相合)——行星互相\"对视\":连星星都在交换眼神。","ad + specere (to look): the gaze aimed at a thing. Astrology's aspect has planets \"looking at\" each other — even stars trade glances."],
  visus:["videre(看)的分词,印欧 weid-(看见→知道):希腊 idea、英语 wise、wit 全是亲戚——在这个词根里,看见就是知道。","Participle of videre, root weid- (to see → to know): Greek idea, English wise and wit are all kin — in this root, seeing IS knowing."],
  retro:["re(向回)+ 方位词尾 -tro:朝后方。俄耳甫斯的悲剧浓缩成这一个副词——冥王只禁止了一个方向。","re (back) + directional -tro: rearward. Orpheus's tragedy compresses into this one adverb — Hades forbade only a direction."],
  gradus:["gradi(迈步)之名词:一步、一级台阶。罗马军令 gradum servare——保持步幅:帝国是一步一步、同一步幅走出来的。","Noun of gradi (to stride): one step, one stair. The army order gradum servare — keep the pace: the empire was walked out step by measured step."],
  passus:["pandere(张开)的分词:两脚张开的一跨。罗马计程:1000 passus=1 英里——帝国的地图用腿画成。","Participle of pandere (to spread): the legs' full stride. A thousand passus made a mile — the empire's map was drawn by legs."],
  cursus:["currere(跑)之名词:奔跑、路线、生涯。cursus honorum 是政途,cursus publicus 是国家邮路——罗马把人生和邮政都看作赛道。","Noun of currere (to run): the run, the route, the career. cursus honorum was politics, cursus publicus the imperial post — Rome saw both life and mail as racetracks."],
  fuga:["印欧 bheug-(逃):希腊 phyge 同根。罗马法有 fuga(流放)、乐理有 fuga(赋格)——逃亡可以是刑罚,也可以是艺术。本游戏 FUGA MYTHICA 取的正是\"逃\"。","Indo-European bheug- (to flee): Greek phyge is kin. Law had fuga (exile), music the fugue — flight can be a sentence or an art. This game's own FUGA is exactly that flight."],
  reditus:["red(回)+ itus(行走):归途。奥德修斯的十年 nostos、俄耳甫斯的功亏一篑——古典文学一半的悲欢都押在\"回程\"这个词上。","red (back) + itus (going): the return. Odysseus's ten-year homecoming, Orpheus's last-step failure — half of classical literature is staked on this word."],
  ascensus:["ad(向上)+ scandere(攀):向上爬。scandere 也生出 scandal(绊脚石)——上升与丑闻同根:爬得越高,越容易被绊。","ad (up) + scandere (to climb). scandere also bred scandal (the stumbling-block) — ascent and scandal share a root: the higher the climb, the easier the trip."],
  descensus:["de(向下)+ scandere:下行。维吉尔名句 facilis descensus Averno——下冥府容易,难的是回来:俄耳甫斯用结局证明了这句诗。","de (down) + scandere. Virgil: facilis descensus Averno — the way down is easy; the coming back is the labor: Orpheus proved the line with his ending."],
  lumen:["与 lux 同根 leuk-,加 -men:发光体、灯、眼睛的光。罗马人称眼睛为 lumina——人脸上的两盏灯。","Root leuk- with -men: the light-giver, lamp, the eye's light. Romans called the eyes lumina — two lamps in a face."],
  claritas:["clarus 本指\"声音嘹亮\",后移到视觉\"明亮\"再到\"著名\"——从耳到眼到名声:清晰是一场跨感官的迁徙。","clarus first meant loud and ringing, then bright, then famous — from ear to eye to renown: clarity migrated across the senses."],
  obscuritas:["ob(覆盖)+ 印欧 (s)keu-(遮蔽,英语 sky 同根!):昏暗=\"被罩住\"。sky 本义是\"云罩\"——天空与昏暗,词根里是一家。","ob (over) + (s)keu- (to cover — kin to sky!): dimness is \"the covered state.\" Sky first meant cloud-cover — heaven and obscurity are one family."],
  dubium:["duo(二)+ -bium:\"两可之间\"。哈姆雷特的 to be or not to be 在拉丁语里早有词形——怀疑,就是站在二的中间。","duo (two) + -bium: \"between two.\" Hamlet's question had a Latin word waiting — to doubt is to stand in the middle of two."],
  cura:["词源不明。罗马寓言:女神 Cura 用泥捏了人,所以人终生归\"忧虑\"所有——海德格尔把这故事写进哲学:人是 Cura 的作品。","Origin unknown. In fable the goddess Cura shaped man from clay, so Care owns us while we live — Heidegger built philosophy on the tale: we are Cura's handiwork."],
  desperatio:["de(丧失)+ sperare(希望):希望的破产。罗马兵法却警告 desperatio 最危险——绝望的敌人战斗力翻倍:连放弃都会反弹。","de (loss of) + sperare (to hope): hope's bankruptcy. Yet Roman tacticians warned nothing fights harder than despair — even surrender can recoil."],
  fiducia:["fides 家族的\"自信托\":对人或己的笃定。罗马法有 fiducia 信托制度——把财产托给信得过的人:金融的地基是这个词根。","Of the fides family: firm reliance on someone or oneself. Roman law's fiducia was the trust arrangement — property placed in trusted hands: finance rests on this root."],
  patientia:["pati(承受)+ -entia:承受的功夫。斯多噶双箴 sustine et abstine——忍住,戒住:patientia 是罗马人给苦难备的铠甲。","pati (to endure) + -entia: the craft of bearing. The Stoic motto: sustine et abstine — bear and forbear: patience was Rome's armor against fortune."],
  festinatio:["festinare(赶)之名词。奥古斯都的座右铭 festina lente(慢慢地快)——皇帝用一个悖论治国:急事,慢做。","Noun of festinare (to hasten). Augustus ruled by the paradox festina lente — make haste slowly: urgent things, done unhurriedly."],
  error:["errare(游荡)之名词:先是\"迷路\",后是\"犯错\"——错误的本义只是走岔了路。errare humanum est:迷路是人的天性,原谅也该是。","Noun of errare (to wander): first \"losing the way,\" then \"being wrong\" — error originally just meant a wrong turn. errare humanum est: wandering is human; so should forgiving be."],
  /* ---- C2 L10 ---- */
  aeternitas:["aevum(时代、寿命)缩合出 aeternus:\"贯穿所有时代的\"。罗马帝国自称 Roma aeterna——永恒之城:城还在,词没吹牛。","From aevum (age, lifetime) came aeternus: \"lasting through all ages.\" Rome styled herself Roma aeterna — the Eternal City: the city stands; the word kept its promise."],
  immortalitas:["im(不)+ mors(死)+ -itas:不死性。西塞罗说著作是 immortalitas 的捷径——写下来,就死不掉:他赌对了,我们还在读他。","im (not) + mors (death) + -itas. Cicero called writing the shortcut to immortality — set it down and it cannot die: he bet right; we still read him."],
  natura:["nasci(诞生)+ -ura:\"将要出生的一切\"。卢克莱修用 De Rerum Natura(物性论)让它成为\"自然\"——自然=万物不断出生的过程。","nasci (to be born) + -ura: \"all that is to be born.\" Lucretius's De Rerum Natura fixed it as Nature — nature is the world perpetually being born."],
  mundus:["本义\"洁净、齐整\"——罗马人译希腊 kosmos(既是宇宙又是妆饰!)时选了它:宇宙=打扫干净的大房间。cosmetics 与 cosmos 同根,mundus 全懂。","First \"clean, neat\" — chosen to translate Greek kosmos (both universe and adornment!): the cosmos is a well-kept room. Cosmetics and cosmos share a root; mundus got the joke."],
  orbis:["词源不明:圆。罗马人说 orbis terrarum(诸地之圆)=全世界——教皇祝福 Urbi et Orbi:一字之差,罗马城与全世界。","A circle of unknown origin. orbis terrarum, \"the circle of lands,\" meant the world — the papal Urbi et Orbi blesses city and globe, one letter apart."],
  principium:["princeps(第一人)+ -ium:开端=\"第一位所在\"。《约翰福音》拉丁文开篇 In principio erat Verbum——太初有道:principium 站在圣经第一句。","princeps (the first one) + -ium: the beginning is where the first stands. The Latin Gospel opens In principio erat Verbum — this word opens Scripture itself."],
  elementum:["词源成谜,一说来自字母 L-M-N 连读——字母是语言的元素,元素是万物的字母:一个词把化学和识字课连在一起。","Origin debated — one theory: the letters L-M-N read aloud. Letters are language's elements; elements are the world's alphabet: one word joins chemistry to the ABC."],
  harmonia:["希腊 harmos(接榫):造船匠的词——木板严丝合缝为 harmonia。乐音相合借的是木工手艺:和声是耳朵里的榫卯。","Greek harmos (a joint): a shipwright's word — planks fitted tight were in harmonia. Music borrowed carpentry: harmony is joinery for the ear."],
  concordia:["con(同)+ cor(心):众心合一。罗马广场立协和神庙 Concordia——内战间隙建的:最缺什么,就给什么立庙。","con (together) + cor (heart): hearts as one. The temple of Concordia rose in the Forum — built between civil wars: Rome raised temples to what it lacked most."],
  discordia:["dis(裂)+ cor(心):心的分裂。不和女神因未获婚宴请柬掷出金苹果,引爆特洛伊战争——史上最贵的一次没发请帖。","dis (apart) + cor (heart): the heart split. Uninvited to a wedding, Discord threw the golden apple that ignited Troy — history's costliest missing invitation."],
  genius:["gignere(生育)家族:与生俱来的守护灵。罗马人生日祭自己的 genius——天才的原义:每人体内住着一位神,生日快乐是对他说的。","Family of gignere (to beget): the guardian spirit born with you. Romans sacrificed to their genius on birthdays — genius first meant the god within; \"happy birthday\" was addressed to him."],
  laurus:["地中海古词。宁芙达佛涅逃避阿波罗化为月桂,阿波罗遂以桂叶为冠——桂冠的每片叶子都是一场未遂的爱情。","An old Mediterranean word. Fleeing Apollo, the nymph Daphne became the laurel; he crowned himself with her leaves — every laurel wreath is a love that got away."],
  praemium:["prae(在先)+ emere(拿取):战利品中\"最先拿的那份\"——统帅先挑。奖赏的本义:第一顺位的挑选权。","prae (before) + emere (to take): the share taken first from the spoils — the commander picked before all. A prize originally meant first pick."],
  monumentum:["monere(提醒)+ -mentum:提醒之物。贺拉斯夸口诗比青铜更久 monumentum aere perennius——两千年过去,青铜锈了,那行诗还在被背诵:他赢了。","monere (to remind) + -mentum: the reminder-thing. Horace boasted his verse a monumentum aere perennius, outlasting bronze — two thousand years on, the bronze is gone and the line is still recited: he won."],
  littera:["或经伊特鲁里亚借自希腊 diphthera(书写用的皮子)——字母的名字本是\"那张皮\":欧洲的读写课,从一块羊皮开始。","Perhaps via Etruscan from Greek diphthera (writing-hide) — the letter's name was once \"the skin\": Europe's literacy began on a piece of leather."],
  scriptor:["scribere(刻写)+ -or:书写本义是\"刻划\"——最早的字是刻进蜡板泥板的。作家至今\"笔耕\":scriptor 一直在刻。","scribere (to scratch) + -or: writing began as incision — letters cut into wax and clay. Writers still \"carve out\" their work: the scriptor never stopped scratching."],
  lector:["legere(拾取→阅读)+ -or:读者=\"拾字的人\"。阅读的拉丁意象是弯腰捡穗——一行行拾起字粒:读书是文字的秋收。","legere (to gather → to read) + -or: the reader is \"the gleaner.\" Latin pictured reading as gathering grain line by line — every book a harvest field."],
  vates:["凯尔特-意大利古词:通灵的歌者。奥古斯都时代诗人自称 vates——不是文人,是先知:维吉尔写罗马的过去,也在预言它的使命。","An old Celto-Italic word: the inspired singer. Augustan poets claimed the title vates — not littérateurs but prophets: Virgil sang Rome's past as its destiny."],
  opus:["印欧 op-(劳作):作品=劳动的结晶。音乐家的 Op.1、杰作 magnum opus——西方给\"工作成果\"编号的传统,从这个词根开始。","Indo-European op- (to work): the work wrought. A composer's Op. 1, the magnum opus — the West's habit of numbering its labors begins at this root."],
  auctor:["augere(使生长)+ -or:作者=\"使之生长者\"。罗马的 auctoritas(权威)同根——权威的本义不是压制,是曾经栽培:说话有分量,因为种过树。","augere (to make grow) + -or: an author is \"one who makes grow.\" Kin auctoritas — authority originally meant not command but cultivation: your word has weight because you have planted."],
};
